\vspace{-1.2em}

\begin{abstract}
\small\noindent
Boosted trees won the M5 forecasting competition, but M5 is one large,
feature-rich dataset, and its ranking need not hold elsewhere. This thesis
asks which measurable properties of a dataset explain when tree-based
ensembles beat simple and statistical benchmarks in daily retail sales
forecasting.

A conditional-mean framework splits sales into a stable store level,
predictable variation and residual noise. Ensembles should gain when the
available features recover variation that a baseline misses. Experiments on
1115 Rossmann stores in 2013--2015 change the panel size, the training
history, the store composition and the feature information. The main
comparisons use fixed-origin 42-day holdouts; the experiments with updated
lags evaluate one-step forecasts separately.

With only their own sales history, XGBoost and LightGBM do worse than a
store-day-of-week baseline and per-store Auto-ARIMA. With calendar, promotion
and store features they cut RMSE by roughly 39\% against that weekly
baseline, and updated sales lags bring the cut close to 50\%. More stores do
not reliably help, and the gain from a longer training history mostly stops
after twelve months.

A covariate-matched comparison on 50 stores at two origins changes this
ranking. Given the same covariates as the trees, ARIMAX is nearly tied with
LightGBM at the first origin, and the trees win at the second; paired
block-bootstrap intervals show the same contrast. With 3, 6 or 12 months of
training history the pattern holds, and ARIMAX is never significantly better
than LightGBM. Covariates close much of ARIMA's gap to the trees, and what
remains depends on period and specification. Models should therefore be
compared under the same information rather than ranked by family.

\par\vspace{1ex}
\noindent\textbf{Key words: } retail demand forecasting, gradient boosting, LightGBM, XGBoost, dataset characteristics, Rossmann store sales, M5 competition.
\end{abstract}

