\section{Introduction}
% ============================================================

The M5 Forecasting Competition showed that gradient-boosted trees, and
especially LightGBM, can do very well on large retail forecasting problems
\citep{makridakis2022conclusions}. Because so many leading entries used these
methods, M5 has become a reference point for later retail forecasting work.
But M5 is one particular setting: daily Walmart product sales in three US
states, with a large cross-section, many zero-sales days at product level, a
long sales history, detailed price and calendar variables, and a single
28-day test period. It shows how LightGBM performs there. It does not tell us
whether the same ranking holds in retail data with a different scale, level
of aggregation, history length or feature set. This thesis asks which part of
that setting actually drives the tree advantage.

\begin{mdframed}[backgroundcolor=gray!8, linecolor=gray!50, linewidth=1pt,
    innertopmargin=10pt, innerbottommargin=10pt, innerrightmargin=10pt, innerleftmargin=10pt]
\textbf{Research Question:} \textit{Which measurable properties of retail
demand data explain when tree-based ensemble methods outperform simple
baselines in retail sales forecasting?}
\end{mdframed}

\medskip
The thesis answers this question with a theoretical framework and experiments
on the Rossmann Store Sales dataset. The framework writes the gap between an
ensemble and a simple baseline in terms of two things: how much predictable
within-store variation there is, and how much of it the observed features
recover. The Rossmann experiments then move these quantities by changing the
number of stores, the length of the training window, the store-type mix and
the information in the feature set. None of these levers is the final
explanation. Each one is a way to change, or reveal, a deeper property of the
data. What should carry over to other datasets is that deeper property: the
predictable variation the baseline leaves behind, how strong the seasonal
benchmark is, and how much recent and recurring demand information the model
can actually see.

\paragraph{From raw records to a forecasting problem.} The raw data is a
long panel of daily rows, one row per store and day. Each row has a date, a
store identifier, the day's total sales and a few flags and attributes, such
as whether the store was open, whether a promotion ran that day, and the
store's format and assortment level (Section~\ref{sec:application} gives the
full list). To answer the research question, this panel is turned into two
objects for every store--day pair $(s,t)$: a target $y_{s,t}$, the quantity
every model tries to predict, and a feature vector $\mathbf{x}_{s,t}$, the
information a model may use to predict it. The baselines and the tree
ensembles differ only in how much of $\mathbf{x}_{s,t}$ they see and how
flexibly they turn it into a prediction $\hat{y}_{s,t}$. The framework in
Section~\ref{sec:framework} makes this precise by splitting $y_{s,t}$ into
the part a model with a given $\mathbf{x}_{s,t}$ could recover and the part
it never could. Seen this way, the research question is not about algorithm
names. It is about what each estimator is shown and what it does with it.
This is also why the estimators in Section~\ref{sec:estimators} are
introduced on one running example instead of as an abstract list.

\subsection*{Contributions}

The thesis makes three contributions. First, it builds a framework for the
performance gap between tree-based ensembles and a store-mean baseline, and
derives testable predictions from two quantities: how much predictable
variation each series has, and how well the observed features recover it.
Second, it applies the framework to daily sales from 1115 Rossmann stores over
roughly 2.5 years. It compares linear regression, a single CART tree, bagging,
Random Forest, XGBoost and LightGBM against store-level Auto-ARIMA, ETS and
Theta. Rossmann is a useful contrast to M5 because it has fewer series, a
shorter history and store-level instead of product-level targets. Third, the
experiments vary the number of stores, the training length, the store-type
mix, the availability of lags and the test period, and then translate the
resulting differences back into measurable data properties, so that the answer
is not tied to conditions specific to Rossmann.

\subsection*{Structure}

Section~\ref{sec:literature} reviews the forecasting literature and the role of
the M competitions. Section~\ref{sec:framework} builds the modelling framework.
It separates the forecasting model from the estimators that try to recover it,
introduces those estimators step by step on a simulated panel, from a single
regression tree to the boosted ensembles, and derives the testable predictions.
Section~\ref{sec:application} applies the framework to the Rossmann data: it
describes the data, sets out the experiments, reports the results and
re-expresses them as data properties. Section~\ref{sec:conclusion} concludes.

% ============================================================
\section{Relation to the Literature}
\label{sec:literature}
% ============================================================

\subsection{The M Competition Series and the Shift Toward Machine Learning}

The M competitions matter here because much of the evidence for tree-based
forecasting comes from them. They evaluate every method on the same datasets,
test periods and accuracy measures, so their results can be compared in a way
that scattered studies cannot. LightGBM's strong result in M5
\citep{makridakis2022conclusions} is the starting point of this thesis: does
that advantage survive when the retail data look different?

The early competitions favoured simple methods. In M1, statistically
sophisticated models could not consistently beat exponential smoothing or naïve
forecasts \citep{makridakis2022conclusions}, and M3 singled out the simple Theta
method as one of its strongest performers across 3003 series
\citep{assimakopoulos2000theta}. M4 marked the turning point. Its winner combined
exponential smoothing with a recurrent neural network, and several
machine-learning methods did well across 100000 series, yet pure machine
learning still trailed statistical combinations on average
\citep{makridakis2020m4}. The balance only changed in M5, where most
of the top 50 Accuracy and Uncertainty submissions used LightGBM, deep neural
networks, or combinations of the two, and beat the statistical benchmarks at
most levels of the hierarchy \citep{makridakis2022conclusions}.

\subsubsection{Why Machine-Learning Performance Changed Across the Competitions}

Algorithms alone do not explain this shift. The tasks themselves changed. The
early competitions were largely univariate series from mixed domains and
frequencies, where a method had to infer level, trend, and seasonality from each
series' own short history. These conditions suit exponential
smoothing, ARIMA, Theta, and forecast combinations. Machine learning also got a
raw deal in how it was applied. Models often treated each series in isolation,
which left few observations to estimate non-linear relationships and a high risk
of fitting noise, and the strongest methods of the period pre-dated the
regularisation, subsampling, and efficient training of modern XGBoost and
LightGBM \citep{chen2016xgboost,ke2017lightgbm}. M4 sat in between. Its 100000
series gave enough scale for global and hybrid learning, but its mix of domains
and thin set of common predictors kept pure machine-learning results uneven
\citep{makridakis2020m4}.

M5 changed both the data and the modelling strategy. Its 30490 bottom-level
series came from one retail system, linked through products, departments,
stores, states, and calendar time, so participants could pool them into global
models and feed in sales lags, rolling statistics, prices, events, and
promotions. Boosted trees suddenly had a large tabular sample of related
commercial behaviour, a setting where learning non-linear interactions
pays off and short isolated series do not. The shift therefore reflects the data
environment as much as the algorithms, and that is what motivates the research
question, since changing the conditions may change the tree-ensemble advantage
with them. M5 is itself only one setting, Walmart sales in the United States, with
prices, promotions, holidays, and a product--store hierarchy full of strong
weekly and intermittent patterns. Its result need not carry to datasets with
fewer series, shorter histories, or sparser features. Neural networks also did
well in M5 but fall outside this thesis, which sticks to XGBoost and LightGBM so
that the data conditions can be studied closely instead of being buried under an
architecture comparison.

\subsection{The M5 Competition: Data, Design, and Findings}

\subsubsection{Data and Competition Design}

The M5 dataset records daily unit sales for 3049 products in 10 Walmart stores
across California, Texas, and Wisconsin. That gives 30490 bottom-level
product--store series, which aggregate through categories, departments, stores,
and states into 42840 series in total. The data span 29 January 2011 to 19 June
2016 (1969 daily observations per series), and participants forecast the next 28
days \citep{makridakis2022m5background}. They also received calendar and event
indicators and product-level prices. Accuracy was measured by the Weighted Root
Mean Squared Scaled Error (WRMSSE), which scales each series' errors by its
historical variation and weights series by dollar sales.
Table~\ref{tab:m5compare} in Section~\ref{sec:data} sets the dataset's
structural properties against those of the data used here.

\subsubsection{The Winning Approach: Global Bottom-Up LightGBM}

Many leading submissions used a global bottom-up approach built on LightGBM
\citep{ma2022robustness}. Instead of one model per series, a single model was
trained on observations pooled across all 30490 bottom-level series, usually with
sales lags, rolling averages, calendar variables, prices, and promotions, and
forecasts were produced at the bottom level and aggregated upward. Pooling let
the model learn effects shared across products and stores, such as weekly
patterns and price responses. Nor was the approach limited to the winning entry:
every top-50 Accuracy team beat the organisers' strongest benchmark by at least 16\%
\citep{makridakis2022m5,ma2022robustness}, and LightGBM featured in most of the
prize-winning Uncertainty submissions producing probabilistic forecasts
\citep{makridakis2022m5uncertainty,ord2022uncertainty}.

\subsubsection{A Qualification: Robustness Across Test Windows}

The official ranking used a single 28-day window, so it cannot say whether the
ordering holds at other origins. \citet{ma2022robustness} re-evaluated leading
LightGBM-based methods over five rolling 28-day windows. Rankings shifted
noticeably even with the dataset and methods held fixed, which means part of the
measured difference rode on the choice of test window. They recommend multiple
rolling test periods when assessing competition results, advice this thesis
follows in Stage~4.

This thesis treats the boosted trees that led M5 as estimators of the
predictable structure in the forecasting model, not as standalone algorithms.
Their mechanics, and the steps that lead from a single regression tree to
XGBoost and LightGBM, are therefore explained inside the framework, in
Section~\ref{sec:estimators}.

\subsection{Tree-Based Methods in Retail Demand Forecasting}

\subsubsection{Why Ensemble Trees Are Useful for Tabular Retail Data}

Retail demand reflects several forces (average store level, weekly and annual
seasonality, promotions, competition, and local shocks) whose effects interact,
so a promotion may matter differently by store type or weekday. Trees pick up
such interactions without anyone specifying them in advance. A tree can split on
store type, then on promotion status and day of week, so that a boosted model
learns that promotions behave differently on Mondays in one store format than
in another, where a linear regression would need the interaction written out by
hand. And because splits depend on the ordering of values, not their
units, lags, binary indicators, calendar fields, and continuous measures such as
competitor distance all enter the same model without standardisation.

Trained globally, a tree model can also borrow information across series
\citep{ma2022robustness}. A store with few promotion periods of its own leans on
other stores' observations when estimating a promotion effect, which helps most
when stores share demand patterns and tails off as their behaviour diverges.

The two implementations used throughout, XGBoost \citep{chen2016xgboost} and
LightGBM \citep{ke2017lightgbm}, are the current standard for this kind of
tabular forecasting. Their mechanics are described in
Section~\ref{sec:estimators}.

\subsection{Global Forecasting Models and Dataset Characteristics}

\paragraph{Global versus local models.} The M5 winners were \emph{global}
models, meaning one model trained on observations pooled across every series.
\citet{monteromanso2021principles} argue that this is more than a convenience.
Given enough complexity, a global model can match or outperform local ones even
when the series are unrelated, because forcing one function to fit many series
at once acts as regularisation. The benefit from pooling grows with the
capacity the global model is given, and capacity is exactly what the boosted
trees in this thesis are tuned on. \citet{januschowski2020criteria} go further
and argue that the global--local split classifies forecasting methods better
than the older machine-learning--statistical split. This is the comparison
made here: global boosted trees against local statistical models fitted per
store.

\paragraph{Why trees keep winning on tabular data.}
\citet{grinsztajn2022tabular} benchmark tree ensembles against deep learning
across a large collection of tabular datasets, and boosted trees remain ahead on
medium-sized problems. They trace this to three features of tabular data:
irregular target functions, uninformative features, and feature spaces that are
not rotation-invariant. A typical store-level retail feature matrix, with its
mix of identifiers, calendar fields, and a handful of continuous variables, fits
that profile almost exactly. So it is not surprising that the strongest M5
entries were tree-based, not neural.

\paragraph{Dataset characteristics and method performance.} A separate strand of
work ties forecasting accuracy to measurable properties of the data.
\citet{kang2017visualising} place series in an instance space spanned by features
such as seasonal strength, trend, and entropy, and show that the best-performing
method changes systematically across that space. \citet{spiliotis2020m}
run the same analysis on the M competition data and find that those datasets
occupy a narrow region of the feature space, which cautions against reading too
much into competition rankings. This thesis takes that caution seriously but
turns the approach around. Instead of comparing methods across many different
datasets, it changes the relevant characteristics experimentally inside one
dataset.

The next section makes the mechanism precise. It models the gap between a
tree-based ensemble and a simple store-level baseline as a function of
measurable properties of the data.

% ============================================================

\section{Model and Framework}
\label{sec:framework}
How accurate a forecast can be depends on three things: the information
available at the forecast origin, the predictable variation the benchmark
leaves behind, and the estimation error that comes from a finite sample. The
framework below puts these together. It organises the mechanisms; the
Rossmann results cannot be used to identify the separate hidden demand
components it describes.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Notation used throughout the thesis.}\label{tab:notation}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lp{10cm}}
\toprule
Symbol & Meaning \\
\midrule
$s,t;N,T$ & Store and target-date indices; panel dimensions \\
$y_{s,t},\mu_s$ & Daily sales and stable store-level mean \\
$\mathcal F_{s,t},\mathbf x_{s,t}$ & Reference origin-available information; selected predictors \\
$\delta_{s,t},u_{s,t}$ & Predictable departure from the mean; residual relative to $\mathcal F$ \\
$\delta^*(\mathbf x),\xi_{s,t}$ & Feature-based conditional mean of $\delta$; its inaccessible part \\
$f,\hat f;\hat\delta$ & Regression function, fitted predictor and fitted departure \\
$\rho_\infty^2,R_{\rm rec}$ & Feature ceiling; realised recovery after estimation error \\
$\mathcal T,\mathcal S,n$ & Training/test store--day pairs; test size \\
$p,v,\tau;M,m$ & Feature count/index and threshold; ensemble size/iteration \\
$\kappa,\sigma^2$ & Correlation and variance across training draws at fixed inputs \\
$\mathcal L,\eta$ & Loss and learning rate \\
$J,j,\mathcal J,I_j,w_j$ & Leaf count/index/set, leaf observations and weight \\
$g,h;L$ & First/second loss derivatives; lag operator \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Forecasting Model: Decomposing Retail Sales}
For a specified origin and horizon, let $\mathcal F_{s,t}$ contain only
information available when the forecast is issued. Write
\begin{equation}
 y_{s,t}=\mu_s+\delta_{s,t}+u_{s,t},\qquad
 \delta_{s,t}=\mathbb E[y_{s,t}\mid\mathcal F_{s,t}]-\mu_s,
 \label{eq:dgp}
\end{equation}
where $\mathbb E[u_{s,t}\mid\mathcal F_{s,t}]=0$. In words, $\mu_s$ is the
store's normal level, $\delta_{s,t}$ is the part of the day's deviation from
that level that could be predicted with the information available at the
origin, and $u_{s,t}$ is everything else. The residual mixes real local effects
and pure noise, and the thesis does not try to separate the two. A local event can
be systematic without being predictable at the origin. What counts as unpredictable therefore depends on the information set:
with more information, part of $u$ moves into $\delta$. Because $\delta$ is
measured around each store's own level, $\mu_s$ can be read as the store's
long-run mean. Treating that level as stable over an evaluation window is an
approximation.

\subsection{Baseline Forecast and Its Error}\label{sec:baseline}
The store-mean benchmark uses the training history:
\begin{equation}
 \hat y^{\rm naive}_{s,t}=\bar y_s=|T_s|^{-1}\sum_{t\in T_s}y_{s,t}.
 \label{eq:naive}
\end{equation}
Its error is
\begin{equation}
 e^{\rm naive}_{s,t}=(\mu_s-\bar y_s)+\delta_{s,t}+u_{s,t}
 \approx\delta_{s,t}+u_{s,t}.\label{eq:naive_error}
\end{equation}
Dropping the level-estimation error $(\mu_s-\bar y_s)$ is least safe when the
history is short or the store's level drifts. Store-DOW, which also averages by
weekday, already removes the weekly part of $\delta$, so it is a harder
benchmark than Store-mean.

\subsection{Tree-Based Forecast and the Performance Gap}
For a fitted training sample, the feature-based prediction is
\begin{equation}
 \hat y^{\rm tree}_{s,t}=\hat\mu_s+\hat\delta(\mathbf x_{s,t}),
 \label{eq:tree_pred}
\end{equation}
with error, when level-estimation error is small,
\begin{equation}
 e^{\rm tree}_{s,t}\approx\delta_{s,t}-\hat\delta(\mathbf x_{s,t})+u_{s,t}.
 \label{eq:tree_error}
\end{equation}
The comparison rests on three assumptions: (A1) the level-estimation errors are
negligible, (A2) the residual has conditional mean zero as in
Equation~\eqref{eq:dgp}, and (A3) both fitting and prediction use only
information available at the origin. Rolling one-step forecasts use updated
information, so they are a different task from fixed-origin 42-day
forecasting. Under these assumptions,
\begin{align}
 {\rm MSE}^{\rm naive}&\approx{\rm Var}(\delta)+\mathbb E[u^2],\label{eq:mse_naive}\\
 {\rm MSE}^{\rm tree}&\approx\mathbb E[(\delta-\hat\delta)^2]+\mathbb E[u^2].\label{eq:mse_tree}
\end{align}
The mean squared error keeps the squared bias of the forecast; using the error
variance instead would drop that term. In general the first term of
Equation~\eqref{eq:mse_naive} is $\mathbb E[\delta^2] = {\rm Var}(\delta) +
(\mathbb E[\delta])^2$; writing ${\rm Var}(\delta)$ assumes that $\delta$ has mean
zero over the evaluation period, which need not hold for a seasonal holdout.
Subtracting the two gives
\begin{equation}
 G={\rm MSE}^{\rm naive}-{\rm MSE}^{\rm tree}
 \approx{\rm Var}(\delta)-\mathbb E[(\delta-\hat\delta)^2].\label{eq:mse_diff}
\end{equation}
For ${\rm Var}(\delta)>0$, define realised recovery by
\begin{equation}
 R_{\rm rec}=1-\frac{\mathbb E[(\delta-\hat\delta)^2]}{{\rm Var}(\delta)},
 \qquad G\approx{\rm Var}(\delta)R_{\rm rec}.\label{eq:gap}
\end{equation}
$R_{\rm rec}$ can be negative: a tree that fits $\delta$ badly does worse than
the store mean. It is also not a squared correlation, because a prediction can
move together with demand and still be biased or badly scaled.
Let $\delta^*(\mathbf x)=\mathbb E[\delta\mid\mathbf x]$ and
$\xi=\delta-\delta^*(\mathbf x)$. Under out-of-sample orthogonality of $\xi$
and the fitted estimation error,
\begin{equation}
 \mathbb E[(\delta-\hat\delta)^2]
 ={\rm Var}(\delta)(1-\rho_\infty^2)+\mathbb E[(\delta^*-\hat\delta)^2],
 \label{eq:gap_decomp}
\end{equation}
where $\rho_\infty^2={\rm Var}(\delta^*)/{\rm Var}(\delta)\in[0,1]$.
Consequently,
\begin{equation}
 G\approx{\rm Var}(\delta)\rho_\infty^2-\mathbb E[(\delta^*-\hat\delta)^2].
 \label{eq:gap_drivers}
\end{equation}
Equation~\eqref{eq:gap_drivers} separates two things. The ceiling
$\rho_\infty^2$ is about information: how much of $\delta$ the features could
explain at best. The second term is about estimation: how close the fitted
model gets to that best. The two can move in opposite directions. More
observations can lower the estimation error, but they can also change the mix
of stores or add older periods that behave differently. Richer features raise
the ceiling, but in a finite sample they do not guarantee a gain. Against
Store-DOW, the room to improve is the variation left after the weekly pattern
is removed. The property regressions later in the thesis measure
associations; they do not identify $\delta$ or test the decomposition causally.


\subsection{The Estimators: From a Single Tree to Boosted Ensembles}
\label{sec:estimators}

<<sim-ladder, include=FALSE, cache=TRUE>>=
# Estimator-ladder simulation (Section 3.4). DGP: y = mu_s + delta + phi + eps
# with an interactive predictable component and a short panel, so estimation
# error is visible. Bias^2/variance measured against the known conditional
# mean on a fixed test panel across 20 training draws.
set.seed(999)
N_lad <- 100; T_lad <- 90; cut_lad <- 76
dow_eff <- rnorm(7); mu_vec <- rnorm(N_lad, 6000, 2000); z_vec <- runif(N_lad, 0, 10)
delta_fun <- function(dow, promo, z)
  dow_eff[dow] + promo * (0.8 + 0.6 * (dow %in% c(5, 6, 7))) + 0.6 * sin(z) + 0.4 * (z > 6)
set.seed(7)
cal_dow <- sample(1:7, 2e5, TRUE); cal_promo <- rbinom(2e5, 1, 0.35)
cal_z <- z_vec[sample(1:N_lad, 2e5, TRUE)]
raw <- delta_fun(cal_dow, cal_promo, cal_z)
A_sc <- 600 / sd(raw); raw_mean <- mean(raw)
build_panel <- function(seed, lo, hi) {
  set.seed(seed); nd <- hi - lo + 1
  store <- rep(1:N_lad, each = nd); t <- rep(lo:hi, N_lad); dow <- ((t - 1) %% 7) + 1
  promo <- rbinom(N_lad * nd, 1, 0.35); z <- z_vec[store]
  delta <- A_sc * (delta_fun(dow, promo, z) - raw_mean)
  X <- cbind(store = store, dow = dow, promo = promo, z = z,
             n1 = rnorm(N_lad * nd), n2 = rnorm(N_lad * nd),
             n3 = rnorm(N_lad * nd), n4 = rnorm(N_lad * nd))
  fstar <- mu_vec[store] + delta
  y <- fstar + rnorm(N_lad * nd, 0, 300) + rnorm(N_lad * nd, 0, 300)
  list(X = X, y = y, fstar = fstar, store = store)
}
te <- build_panel(123, cut_lad + 1, T_lad)
R_REPS <- 20
mods <- c("cart_shallow", "cart_deep", "bagging", "rf", "xgboost", "lightgbm")
pred_arr <- setNames(lapply(mods, function(m) matrix(NA_real_, R_REPS, length(te$y))), mods)
kap_b <- kap_r <- numeric(R_REPS)
for (r in 1:R_REPS) {
  tr <- build_panel(1000 + r, 1, cut_lad)
  trd <- as.data.frame(tr$X); trd$y <- tr$y; ted <- as.data.frame(te$X)
  fit <- rpart(y ~ ., trd, control = rpart.control(cp = 1e-5, minsplit = 10, maxdepth = 30, xval = 0))
  pred_arr$cart_deep[r, ] <- predict(fit, ted)
  fit <- rpart(y ~ ., trd, control = rpart.control(cp = 0, minsplit = 2, maxdepth = 3, xval = 0))
  pred_arr$cart_shallow[r, ] <- predict(fit, ted)
  fit <- ranger(y ~ ., trd, num.trees = 200, mtry = 8, min.node.size = 10, num.threads = 4)
  pr <- predict(fit, ted, predict.all = TRUE)$predictions
  pred_arr$bagging[r, ] <- rowMeans(pr)
  cc <- cor(pr[, 1:40]); kap_b[r] <- mean(cc[upper.tri(cc)])
  fit <- ranger(y ~ ., trd, num.trees = 200, mtry = 3, min.node.size = 10, num.threads = 4)
  pr <- predict(fit, ted, predict.all = TRUE)$predictions
  pred_arr$rf[r, ] <- rowMeans(pr)
  cc <- cor(pr[, 1:40]); kap_r[r] <- mean(cc[upper.tri(cc)])
  bst <- xgb.train(list(max_depth=6, eta=0.1, subsample=0.8, colsample_bytree=0.8, objective="reg:squarederror", nthread=4), xgb.DMatrix(tr$X,label=tr$y), nrounds=300, verbose=0)
  pred_arr$xgboost[r, ] <- predict(bst, xgb.DMatrix(te$X))
  dl <- lgb.Dataset(tr$X, label = tr$y)
  bst <- lgb.train(list(objective = "regression", num_leaves = 63,
                        learning_rate = 0.05, num_threads = 4, verbosity = -1),
                   dl, nrounds = 300, verbose = -1)
  pred_arr$lightgbm[r, ] <- predict(bst, te$X)
}
lad <- map_dfr(mods, function(m) {
  P <- pred_arr[[m]]
  tibble(model = m,
         rmse  = sqrt(mean(sweep(P, 2, te$y, "-")^2)),
         bias2 = mean((colMeans(P) - te$fstar)^2) / 1000,
         varc  = mean(colMeans(sweep(P, 2, colMeans(P), "-")^2)) / 1000)
}) %>% mutate(esterr = bias2 + varc)
kap_bag <- mean(kap_b); kap_rf <- mean(kap_r)
rmse_v <- setNames(round(lad$rmse), lad$model)
bias_v <- setNames(round(lad$bias2), lad$model)
var_v  <- setNames(round(lad$varc), lad$model)
err_v  <- setNames(round(lad$esterr), lad$model)
lad_lab <- c(cart_shallow = "CART (depth 3)",
             cart_deep = "CART (deep)", bagging = "Bagging (M=200)",
             rf = "Random Forest", xgboost = "XGBoost", lightgbm = "LightGBM")

# ---- Paired significance tests across the R_REPS training draws ----
# Each replication gives one MSE per model on the SAME fixed test panel, so
# pairing removes across-replication noise and isolates the estimator effect
# (same logic as a Diebold-Mariano test, but paired over replications instead
# of over time). H0: E[MSE_A - MSE_B] = 0; H1: E[MSE_A - MSE_B] != 0.
per_rep_mse <- sapply(mods, function(m) {
  P <- pred_arr[[m]]
  rowMeans(sweep(P, 2, te$y, "-")^2)
})
colnames(per_rep_mse) <- mods

seq_pairs <- list(c("cart_shallow","cart_deep"), c("cart_deep","bagging"),
                  c("bagging","rf"), c("rf","xgboost"), c("xgboost","lightgbm"))
seq_test <- map_dfr(seq_pairs, function(p) {
  a <- per_rep_mse[, p[1]]; b <- per_rep_mse[, p[2]]
  tt <- t.test(a, b, paired = TRUE)
  tibble(from = p[1], to = p[2], t = unname(tt$statistic),
         df = unname(tt$parameter), p_raw = tt$p.value)
})
seq_t <- setNames(sprintf("%.2f", seq_test$t), paste(seq_test$from, seq_test$to, sep = "__"))
seq_p <- setNames(seq_test$p_raw, paste(seq_test$from, seq_test$to, sep = "__"))
# fmt_p_eq(): for inline sentences, used as "$p \Sexpr{fmt_p_eq(x)}$" -> "$p < 0.001$" / "$p = 0.032$"
fmt_p_eq <- function(p) if (p < 0.001) "< 0.001" else sprintf("= %.3f", p)
# fmt_p_cell(): for table cells, self-contained math -> "$<0.001$" / "$0.032$"
fmt_p_cell <- function(p) if (p < 0.001) "$<0.001$" else sprintf("$%.3f$", p)

pair_combn <- combn(mods, 2, simplify = FALSE)
full_test <- map_dfr(pair_combn, function(p) {
  a <- per_rep_mse[, p[1]]; b <- per_rep_mse[, p[2]]
  tt <- t.test(a, b, paired = TRUE)
  tibble(A = p[1], B = p[2], mean_diff = mean(a - b),
         t = unname(tt$statistic), p_raw = tt$p.value)
}) %>% mutate(p_holm = p.adjust(p_raw, method = "holm"),
             sig = case_when(p_holm < .001 ~ "***", p_holm < .01 ~ "**",
                             p_holm < .05 ~ "*", TRUE ~ ""))
pl_df <- lad %>%
  transmute(est = factor(lad_lab[model], levels = unname(lad_lab)),
            "Bias^2" = bias2, Variance = varc) %>%
  pivot_longer(-est, names_to = "comp", values_to = "v")
p_lad <- ggplot(pl_df, aes(est, pmax(v, 0.1), fill = comp)) +
  geom_col(position = "dodge") + scale_y_log10() +
  scale_fill_manual(values = c("#4477AA", "#EE6677")) +
  labs(x = NULL, y = "Estimation error component (10^3 sales^2, log scale)", fill = NULL) +
  theme_minimal(base_size = 12) +
  theme(axis.text.x = element_text(angle = 20, hjust = 1), legend.position = "top")
ggsave("eda_outputs/fig_sim_ladder.pdf", p_lad, width = 8, height = 4.2)

# ---- One figure per estimator -------------------------------------------
# Each subsection of Section 3.4 argues that a method exists because the one
# before it leaves a particular component of the estimation error large. These
# figures make that step visible: the current estimator's squared bias and
# variance are shown against those of its predecessor in the sequence, so the
# reader sees which of the two the step moved and which it did not.
seq_order <- c("cart_shallow", "cart_deep", "bagging", "rf", "xgboost", "lightgbm")
step_fig <- function(cur) {
  i   <- match(cur, seq_order)
  prv <- if (i > 1) seq_order[i - 1] else NULL
  d <- lad %>% filter(model %in% c(prv, cur)) %>%
    transmute(model,
              est = factor(lad_lab[model], levels = unname(lad_lab[c(prv, cur)])),
              role = ifelse(model == cur, "This estimator", "Previous step"),
              "Bias^2" = bias2, Variance = varc) %>%
    pivot_longer(c("Bias^2", "Variance"), names_to = "comp", values_to = "v")
  ggplot(d, aes(comp, pmax(v, 0.1), fill = role)) +
    geom_col(position = position_dodge(width = 0.7), width = 0.62) +
    geom_text(aes(label = sprintf("%.0f", v)),
              position = position_dodge(width = 0.7),
              vjust = -0.35, size = 3.1) +
    scale_y_log10(expand = expansion(mult = c(0, 0.22))) +
    scale_fill_manual(values = c("Previous step" = "#BBBBBB",
                                 "This estimator" = "#4477AA")) +
    labs(x = NULL, y = expression("10"^3~"sales"^2~"(log scale)"), fill = NULL) +
    theme_minimal(base_size = 11) +
    theme(legend.position = "top", panel.grid.major.x = element_blank())
}
# The shallow tree opens the sequence and has no predecessor to be compared
# with, so the CART figure is the deep tree against it.
for (m in c("cart_deep", "bagging", "rf", "xgboost", "lightgbm"))
  ggsave(sprintf("eda_outputs/fig_sim_%s.pdf", m), step_fig(m),
         width = 5.2, height = 3.0)
@


The estimation error in Equation~\eqref{eq:gap_drivers} is itself a
bias--variance trade-off,
$\mathbb{E}[(\delta^{*} - \hat{\delta})^2] = \mathrm{Bias}(\hat{\delta})^2
+ \mathrm{Var}(\hat{\delta})$, and the estimators compared in this thesis
differ in which of the two terms they attack. This subsection introduces them
as a connected sequence, not a list: each method exists because the one
before it leaves one part of the estimation error large, and the sequence
moves step by step toward estimators that keep both terms small.

Throughout, the notation is that of the forecasting model. The target is
always daily sales $y_{s,t}$ and the input is always the feature vector
$\mathbf{x}_{s,t}$. Each estimator therefore produces a fit
$\hat{f}(\mathbf{x}_{s,t})$ of the regression function
$f(\mathbf{x}_{s,t}) = \mu_s + \mathbb E[\delta_{s,t}\mid\mathbf{x}_{s,t}]$,
which equals $\mu_s + \delta_{s,t}$ in Equation~\eqref{eq:dgp} only when the
features reveal $\delta_{s,t}$ fully, and the estimate $\hat{\delta}(\mathbf{x}_{s,t})$ in
Equation~\eqref{eq:tree_pred} is this fit net of the store level. Training
observations are the store--day pairs $(s,t) \in \mathcal{T}$.

\paragraph{A running simulation.}
To make each step concrete, every estimator in the sequence is applied to the
same simulated panel, generated from Equation~\eqref{eq:dgp} with every
component known. The panel has $N = 100$ stores and $T = 90$ days (the last 14
days held out) and store levels $\mu_s \sim \mathcal{N}(6000, 2000^2)$.
The parameters were chosen to represent a sales scale broadly similar to
Rossmann, rather than to provide an exact empirical calibration. Its
predictable component $\delta_{s,t}$ has standard deviation 600 and is built
from a day-of-week pattern, a promotion whose lift strengthens toward the
weekend, and a continuous store attribute with a non-linear effect. The residual sums two independent Gaussian draws of standard deviation 300,
so its standard deviation is $300\sqrt{2}$. No serially correlated latent
component is simulated. Four irrelevant noise features are appended to
$\mathbf{x}_{s,t}$. The panel is deliberately
small and its $\delta$ deliberately interactive, so that estimation error is
visible instead of washed out by data volume. Because the conditional mean
$f(\mathbf{x})$ is known, the estimation error of each method can be split
into Monte Carlo estimates of squared bias and variance on a fixed test panel, averaging over
20 independent training draws. Table~\ref{tab:sim_ladder} and
Figure~\ref{fig:sim_ladder} collect the results that the following paragraphs
walk through.

\paragraph{Relation to Rossmann.}
The simulation keeps the main features of the Rossmann data: store
differences, weekly effects and promotion interactions. It is still not a copy
of Rossmann. It has 100 stores and 90 consecutive days instead of 1115 stores
and 2.5 years, every store is observed every day, promotions are drawn
independently and the noise is Gaussian. Its 14-day holdout is also shorter
than the 42-day windows used later. The simulation is there to show the
mechanisms, not to confirm the empirical ranking. Plain gradient boosting is
explained below but is not fitted as a separate estimator in this six-step
illustration.

\paragraph{Why these implementations.} Each estimator below is fitted with
one specific R package, chosen so that the sequence isolates the mechanism it
is meant to illustrate rather than differences in implementation quality.
CART uses \texttt{rpart} \citep{therneau2023rpart}, the standard R implementation of
\citet{breiman1984classification}'s original algorithm. Bagging and the Random Forest both
use \texttt{ranger} \citep{wright2017ranger}, a single
package whose only difference between the two is the \texttt{mtry} feature-subsampling
argument (Section~\ref{sec:rf}). Running both from the same codebase
rules out a library-specific explanation for their gap. \texttt{ranger} was
preferred here over the classic \texttt{randomForest} package because it
parallelises across trees, which matters once $M=200$ trees are grown on
every training draw in the sequence and again across the Rossmann experiments
of Section~\ref{sec:application}. XGBoost and LightGBM use their own
official R packages, \texttt{xgboost} and
\texttt{lightgbm}, maintained by the original authors and cited here through
the same algorithm papers already used above, \citet{chen2016xgboost} and
\citet{ke2017lightgbm}, since neither package has a separate methods paper.
These are the reference implementations, but comparing them still mixes
differences in tuning with differences in the algorithms. The fitted LightGBM
also uses standard gradient boosting (GBDT) without GOSS sampling, so none of
its performance can be credited to GOSS.

\subsubsection{Regression Trees}

\citet{breiman1984classification} introduced Classification and Regression
Trees (CART). A regression tree splits the input space through a sequence of
binary partitions. Every observation in a terminal region receives that
region's mean response. At each internal node the algorithm searches every
feature $x_v$, $v \in \{1,\dots,p\}$, and every candidate threshold $\tau$. It
divides the data into $R_1 = \{\mathbf{x} : x_v \leq \tau\}$ and
$R_2 = \{\mathbf{x} : x_v > \tau\}$, and it keeps the pair $(v,\tau)$ that
minimises the \textbf{residual sum of squares (RSS)} across the two child
nodes:

\begin{equation}
    \text{RSS}(v, \tau) =
    \sum_{(s,t) \in \mathcal{T}:\, \mathbf{x}_{s,t} \in R_1}
    \!\!\left(y_{s,t} - \bar{y}_{R_1}\right)^2
    + \sum_{(s,t) \in \mathcal{T}:\, \mathbf{x}_{s,t} \in R_2}
    \!\!\left(y_{s,t} - \bar{y}_{R_2}\right)^2
    \label{eq:rss_split}
\end{equation}

where $\bar{y}_{R_1}, \bar{y}_{R_2}$ are the mean responses in the child nodes.
The search is exhaustive over features and thresholds, but it is greedy over
depth, because it never asks what a split implies two levels below. Each child
node then repeats the same search from scratch, so different branches can use
different features. Splitting recurses until a stopping rule halts it, such as
a maximum depth or a minimum node size. An observation reaches its leaf only
after a chain of conditional splits. A tree therefore encodes interactions
without anyone specifying them in advance.

\paragraph{Depth changes approximation and sampling error.}
A depth-three tree has at most eight leaves. That is far too few to separate
100 different store levels, let alone the interactions on top of them, so its
squared bias is large. A deeper tree removes most of that bias and lowers the
test error. Textbooks usually say that a deeper tree trades lower bias for
higher variance, but this is not a rule that holds at every depth. On this
panel the measured variance actually falls, from
\Sexpr{var_v['cart_shallow']} to \Sexpr{var_v['cart_deep']} (in $10^3$
sales$^2$).

Even a shallow tree can change from one training draw to the next. When two
splits score almost the same, a small change in the data flips which one is
chosen. Deep trees add more variation on top of that, from small leaves and
unstable splits further down. Bagging, the next step, asks whether averaging
can remove this remaining error. Throughout the sequence the interpretation
follows the measured bias and variance, not an assumed trade-off.

\begin{mdframed}[backgroundcolor=gray!6, linecolor=gray!50, linewidth=0.6pt,
    innertopmargin=6pt, innerbottommargin=6pt, innerrightmargin=8pt, innerleftmargin=8pt]
\textbf{Result on the simulated panel (CART).} The deep tree reaches RMSE $= \Sexpr{rmse_v['cart_deep']}$, with squared bias $\Sexpr{bias_v['cart_deep']}$ and variance $\Sexpr{var_v['cart_deep']}$ ($10^3$ sales$^2$), against RMSE $= \Sexpr{rmse_v['cart_shallow']}$ for the depth-three tree, whose squared bias of $\Sexpr{bias_v['cart_shallow']}$ dwarfs every other entry in Table~\ref{tab:sim_ladder}. Depth removes most of the squared bias and here also lowers the variance. The deep tree still has much more variance than the ensembles that follow.
\end{mdframed}

\begin{figure}[H]
\centering
\IfFileExists{eda_outputs/fig_sim_cart_deep.pdf}{%
  \includegraphics[width=0.62\textwidth]{fig_sim_cart_deep.pdf}}{%
  \fbox{\parbox{0.62\textwidth}{\centering\vspace{1.4em}[Figure placeholder:
  squared bias and variance for this step.]\vspace{1.4em}}}}
\caption{The deep tree against the depth-three tree. Depth sharply reduces squared bias; measured variance also falls on this panel.}
\label{fig:sim_cart}
\end{figure}

\subsubsection{Bagging}

\citet{breiman1996bagging} introduced Bootstrap Aggregating (bagging). The
idea is simple. Instead of one deep tree, grow many deep trees and average
their predictions,
\begin{equation}
    \hat{f}_{\text{bag}}(\mathbf{x})
    = \frac{1}{M}\sum_{m=1}^{M}\hat{f}^{*(m)}(\mathbf{x}),
    \label{eq:bagging}
\end{equation}
where $\hat{f}^{*(m)}$ is the tree grown on the $m$-th sample. The average
works because the trees share a target but not their errors. Every tree
estimates the same regression function, so the signal survives the average.
The noise each tree picks up from its own sample is its own, so it partly
cancels.

The trees have to differ for this to help. CART is deterministic, so trees
grown on the same data would come out identical and averaging them would
change nothing. Each tree is therefore grown on a bootstrap sample, drawn from
the training set with replacement. It sees roughly 63\% of the distinct
training observations, and its neighbours see a different subset.

The trees stay deep. Bagging is aimed at variance, although training on
bootstrap samples and averaging can also change the bias a little
\citep{james2021introduction}.

On the simulated panel, bagging $M = 200$ deep trees cuts the deep tree's
variance sharply, leaves the squared bias of the same small order, and
improves the test RMSE (Table~\ref{tab:sim_ladder}). The gain is still smaller
than it could be. If the trees were independent, averaging would cut their
common variance $\sigma^2$ to $\sigma^2/M$. They are not independent. The
average pairwise correlation between their test predictions, reported as
$\hat{\kappa}$ in Table~\ref{tab:sim_ladder}, is close to one, because every
tree recovers the store levels that drive most of the variation in this panel.
This correlation is measured across test rows, so it mostly reflects the
shared store levels. It is not the correlation of the trees' estimation errors
that matters for the variance in the next subsection.

\begin{mdframed}[backgroundcolor=gray!6, linecolor=gray!50, linewidth=0.6pt,
    innertopmargin=6pt, innerbottommargin=6pt, innerrightmargin=8pt, innerleftmargin=8pt]
\textbf{Result on the simulated panel (Bagging).} Averaging $M=200$ deep trees brings the RMSE down to $\Sexpr{rmse_v['bagging']}$. The variance falls from $\Sexpr{var_v['cart_deep']}$ (deep CART) to $\Sexpr{var_v['bagging']}$. The squared bias rises, from $\Sexpr{bias_v['cart_deep']}$ to $\Sexpr{bias_v['bagging']}$, but much less than the variance falls, so the total estimation error drops from $\Sexpr{err_v['cart_deep']}$ to $\Sexpr{err_v['bagging']}$. The correlation across test rows is about $\hat{\kappa}\approx\Sexpr{sprintf('%.2f', kap_bag)}$. It mostly reflects the store levels every tree picks up, not the error correlation across training draws in Equation~\eqref{eq:var_corr}.
\end{mdframed}

\begin{figure}[H]
\centering
\IfFileExists{eda_outputs/fig_sim_bagging.pdf}{%
  \includegraphics[width=0.62\textwidth]{fig_sim_bagging.pdf}}{%
  \fbox{\parbox{0.62\textwidth}{\centering\vspace{1.4em}[Figure placeholder:
  squared bias and variance for this step.]\vspace{1.4em}}}}
\caption{Bagging against the deep tree. Averaging cuts variance while increasing squared bias modestly in absolute terms.}
\label{fig:sim_bagging}
\end{figure}

\subsubsection{Random Forests}
\label{sec:rf}

That correlation is the next target. For $M$ predictors with pairwise
correlation $\kappa$ and common variance $\sigma^2$, the variance of their
average is
\begin{equation}
    \mathrm{Var}\!\left(\frac{1}{M}\sum_{m=1}^{M} \hat{f}^{*(m)}(\mathbf{x})\right)
    = \kappa\,\sigma^2 + \frac{1-\kappa}{M}\,\sigma^2 .
    \label{eq:var_corr}
\end{equation}
The second term vanishes as $M$ grows. The first does not. Once the trees are
strongly correlated, $\kappa\sigma^2$ is a floor that no number of extra trees
can lower. (Here $\kappa$ and $\sigma^2$ describe the predictions for the same
input $\mathbf{x}$ across different training draws.)

\citet{breiman2001random} addressed this with the Random Forest. It adds a
second source of randomness on top of the bootstrap. At each node the
algorithm draws a random subset of $p^{*} < p$ features and searches only
those, a common regression heuristic being $p^{*} \approx p/3$
\citep{james2021introduction}. The draw is repeated at every node, so a
feature that is unavailable at one split can still be used at the next. The
bootstrap subsamples the rows and this subsamples the columns. A strong
predictor can no longer dominate every tree, and $\kappa$ falls.

The simulation shows the intended effect and its cost together. The measured
correlation $\hat{\kappa}$ falls to a fraction of its bagging value, and the
variance edges below bagging's. The squared bias jumps far enough that the
total estimation error ends up worse than bagging's
(Table~\ref{tab:sim_ladder}). The panel explains why. One feature, the store
identifier, carries most of the signal, and with $p^{*} \approx p/3$ it is
absent from the candidate set at roughly two thirds of the splits. Those
splits fall back on features that carry little. The trees are individually
worse even though they are collectively more diverse. Decorrelation only pays
when the hidden features have good substitutes. One could force the store
identifier into every candidate set, but that was not tested here, and it
would still not turn the forest into bagging, because the other features
would still be subsampled. The same ordering, bagging ahead of the random
forest, comes back in the empirical Stage~1 ranking in
Section~\ref{sec:application}.

\begin{mdframed}[backgroundcolor=gray!6, linecolor=gray!50, linewidth=0.6pt,
    innertopmargin=6pt, innerbottommargin=6pt, innerrightmargin=8pt, innerleftmargin=8pt]
\textbf{Result on the simulated panel (Random Forest).} Feature subsampling lowers the tree correlation to $\hat{\kappa}\approx\Sexpr{sprintf('%.2f', kap_rf)}$, below bagging's $\Sexpr{sprintf('%.2f', kap_bag)}$, but total RMSE rises to $\Sexpr{rmse_v['rf']}$, worse than bagging's $\Sexpr{rmse_v['bagging']}$: squared bias climbs to $\Sexpr{bias_v['rf']}$ while variance falls only to $\Sexpr{var_v['rf']}$. Decorrelating the trees cost more in bias than it saved in variance on this panel, consistent with the cost of sometimes excluding the dominant store identifier.
\end{mdframed}

\begin{figure}[H]
\centering
\IfFileExists{eda_outputs/fig_sim_rf.pdf}{%
  \includegraphics[width=0.62\textwidth]{fig_sim_rf.pdf}}{%
  \fbox{\parbox{0.62\textwidth}{\centering\vspace{1.4em}[Figure placeholder:
  squared bias and variance for this step.]\vspace{1.4em}}}}
\caption{The random forest against bagging. Feature subsampling lowers the
variance a little further and raises the squared bias by more.}
\label{fig:sim_rf}
\end{figure}

\subsubsection{Gradient Boosting}

Bagging and the random forest both reach low bias the expensive way. They grow
each tree deep and then pay for the variance that depth creates. Gradient
boosting takes the opposite route \citep{friedman2001greedy}. Every tree is
kept shallow, so its variance is small from the start, and the bias is removed
sequentially instead of within one tree. Each new tree fits what the ensemble
built so far still gets wrong.

The procedure is gradient descent in function space. For a differentiable
loss $\mathcal{L}(y, \hat{f}(\mathbf{x}))$, the target of iteration $m$ is the
negative gradient of the loss with respect to the current prediction, known in
the boosting literature as the pseudo-residual:

\begin{equation}
    \tilde{r}_{s,t}^{(m)} = -\left[
        \frac{\partial\, \mathcal{L}\!\left(y_{s,t},\, \hat{f}^{(m-1)}(\mathbf{x}_{s,t})\right)}
        {\partial\, \hat{f}^{(m-1)}(\mathbf{x}_{s,t})}
    \right]
    \label{eq:pseudoresid2}
\end{equation}

Here $\hat{f}^{(m-1)}(\mathbf{x}_{s,t})$ is the prediction of the whole
ensemble after $m-1$ rounds, not of any single tree. The negative gradient
points in the direction that prediction should move to lower the loss. Under
squared-error loss it collapses to the ordinary residual
$y_{s,t} - \hat{f}^{(m-1)}(\mathbf{x}_{s,t})$. Other losses, such as absolute
error, Poisson deviance, or quantile loss, change the residuals but leave the
procedure intact.

Each iteration fits a shallow tree $f_m$ to these residuals. That tree is the
descent step. It is then added to the ensemble, scaled by a \textbf{shrinkage
parameter} (learning rate) $\eta \in (0, 1]$:

\begin{equation}
    \hat{f}^{(m)}(\mathbf{x}) = \hat{f}^{(m-1)}(\mathbf{x})
    + \eta\, f_m(\mathbf{x})
    \label{eq:gbm_update}
\end{equation}

A smaller $\eta$ makes each tree contribute less, so more rounds are needed,
and it usually generalises better. Every tree carries the noise of its own fit
alongside the signal. Adding trees at full weight writes that noise into the
model. Shrinkage keeps each update small. Together with the number of rounds
and the tree size, it controls how much the model overfits. Unlike bagging,
boosting does not rely on noise cancelling out across trees.

Where bagging averages away the noise of deep trees, boosting accumulates the
signal of shallow ones: the depth-three tree that was hopeless on its own
becomes the building block of the strongest estimators in the sequence.

\subsubsection{XGBoost}

Plain gradient boosting uses the slope of the loss, which tells the model
which way to move. XGBoost also uses the curvature, which tells it how far
\citep{chen2016xgboost}. At each iteration it minimises a regularised
second-order approximation of the loss:

\begin{equation}
    \mathcal{L}^{(m)} \approx \sum_{(s,t) \in \mathcal{T}}
    \left[g_{s,t}\, f_m(\mathbf{x}_{s,t}) + \tfrac{1}{2}\, h_{s,t}\, f_m(\mathbf{x}_{s,t})^2\right]
    + \Omega(f_m)
    \label{eq:xgboost_obj2}
\end{equation}

Here $g_{s,t}$ and $h_{s,t}$ are the first- and second-order derivatives of
the loss at the current prediction, and $f_m(\mathbf{x}_{s,t})$ is the output
of the new tree. The trees are still shallow and still grown level by level.
What changes is how a split and a leaf value are scored.

The second change is the penalty
$\Omega(f_m)=\gamma J+\frac{1}{2}\lambda\lVert w\rVert^2$, with $J$ the number
of leaves and $w$ the vector of leaf weights. It pushes back against too many
leaves and against large leaf values. The penalty sits inside the objective
instead of being applied afterwards as pruning, so a split is taken only when
its gain exceeds the cost it adds.

Together these give a closed-form optimal weight for each leaf $j$:
\begin{equation}
    w_j^* = -\frac{\sum_{(s,t) \in I_j} g_{s,t}}{\sum_{(s,t) \in I_j} h_{s,t} + \lambda}
    \label{eq:xgb_leaf}
\end{equation}

where $I_j$ holds the training observations in leaf $j$. The numerator is the
total correction the leaf is asked to make, and $\lambda$ sits in the
denominator. A leaf holding few observations has a small Hessian sum, so
$\lambda$ dominates and the leaf value is shrunk toward zero. Small and
unstable leaves are quietened, and a single CART tree has no mechanism of this
kind.

XGBoost also handles missing values during split selection, supports feature
subsampling, and relies on data structures built for parallel computation.
Together these made gradient boosting practical for large tabular datasets.

On the simulated panel the regularised boosting route pays off as the
bias--variance logic predicts. Squared bias and variance are held small at the
same time (Table~\ref{tab:sim_ladder}).

\begin{mdframed}[backgroundcolor=gray!6, linecolor=gray!50, linewidth=0.6pt,
    innertopmargin=6pt, innerbottommargin=6pt, innerrightmargin=8pt, innerleftmargin=8pt]
\textbf{Result on the simulated panel (XGBoost).} RMSE $= \Sexpr{rmse_v['xgboost']}$, squared bias $\Sexpr{bias_v['xgboost']}$, variance $\Sexpr{var_v['xgboost']}$: the first estimator in the sequence to hold both terms down at once, undercutting every averaging-based method above (bagging's total estimation error is $\Sexpr{err_v['bagging']}$, XGBoost's is $\Sexpr{err_v['xgboost']}$). Shallow, sequentially fitted trees with a regularised objective reach a bias--variance combination none of the deep-tree ensembles do.
\end{mdframed}

\begin{figure}[H]
\centering
\IfFileExists{eda_outputs/fig_sim_xgboost.pdf}{%
  \includegraphics[width=0.62\textwidth]{fig_sim_xgboost.pdf}}{%
  \fbox{\parbox{0.62\textwidth}{\centering\vspace{1.4em}[Figure placeholder:
