\subsubsection{LightGBM}

\citet{ke2017lightgbm} built LightGBM to make gradient boosting faster on
large datasets. It changes three things: Gradient-based One-Side Sampling
(GOSS), Exclusive Feature Bundling (EFB), and leaf-wise tree growth.

GOSS reduces the number of rows used to score a split. A large gradient means
the model still fits that observation poorly, so those rows are the ones with
something left to teach. GOSS keeps all of them and takes a sample of the
rest. Writing $\mathcal{A}$ for the rows kept and $\mathcal{B}$ for the rows
sampled, the gradient sum for region $j$ is approximated as:

\begin{equation}
    \tilde{G}_j =
    \sum_{(s,t) \in \mathcal{A}_j} g_{s,t}
    + \frac{1-a}{r} \sum_{(s,t) \in \mathcal{B}_j} g_{s,t}
    \label{eq:goss_gradient}
\end{equation}

where $a$ is the retained fraction of all rows and $r$ is the sampled
low-gradient row count divided by the total row count. Using
only part of the low-gradient rows would make the sum too small, so the factor
$(1-a)/r$ scales that part back up. The sum then stays roughly unbiased, and
splits can be scored on fewer rows.

EFB reduces the number of columns. Some features are almost never non-zero at
the same time. Category indicators are the clearest case, because a product in
one category is not in another. Two such features cannot be confused with each
other, so LightGBM stores them in a single column. If the features are
perfectly exclusive, nothing is lost; if some overlap is allowed, the bundle is
an approximation. Either way there are fewer columns to scan.

Leaf-wise growth changes the tree shape. XGBoost, as configured here, grows
trees depth-wise: it works through the tree level by level, although the
stopping rules do not force every branch to the same depth. LightGBM instead
always splits the leaf that promises the largest drop in loss.
With $\mathcal{J}$ the set of leaves of the current tree, it
selects:

\begin{equation}
    j^* = \arg\max_{j \in \mathcal{J}} \Delta \mathcal{L}_{j}
    \label{eq:leafwise_growth}
\end{equation}

where $\Delta \mathcal{L}_{j}$ is the loss reduction from splitting leaf $j$.
The trees come out asymmetric. Some branches go deep and others stop early, so
the tree spends its effort where the errors are largest. This overfits easily,
because all the leaves can go into one region and fit its noise. The number of
leaves, the minimum leaf size, the learning rate and the regularisation all
still need watching.

On the simulated panel the two boosted implementations close the sequence with
the smallest estimation errors (Table~\ref{tab:sim_ladder}).

\begin{mdframed}[backgroundcolor=gray!6, linecolor=gray!50, linewidth=0.6pt,
    innertopmargin=6pt, innerbottommargin=6pt, innerrightmargin=8pt, innerleftmargin=8pt]
\textbf{Result on the simulated panel (LightGBM).} LightGBM ends with RMSE $= \Sexpr{rmse_v['lightgbm']}$, squared bias $\Sexpr{bias_v['lightgbm']}$ and variance $\Sexpr{var_v['lightgbm']}$. Its total estimation error ($\Sexpr{err_v['lightgbm']}$) is within a few points of XGBoost's ($\Sexpr{err_v['xgboost']}$) and the lowest, or tied for lowest, in the sequence. Starting from the deep tree, whose total error is $\Sexpr{err_v['cart_deep']}$, the sequence more than halves the estimation error by the time it reaches the two boosted models. Whether LightGBM's small edge over XGBoost is real or just replication noise can be read off Table~\ref{tab:sim_pairwise} (row XGBoost vs LightGBM): $t=\Sexpr{seq_t['xgboost__lightgbm']}$, $p \Sexpr{fmt_p_eq(seq_p['xgboost__lightgbm'])}$.
\end{mdframed}

