\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\IfFileExists{eda_outputs/fig_sim_ladder.pdf}{%
  \includegraphics[width=0.78\textwidth]{fig_sim_ladder.pdf}}{%
  \fbox{\parbox{0.9\textwidth}{\centering\vspace{2em}[Figure placeholder:
  bias--variance decomposition of the estimator sequence on the simulated
  panel.]\vspace{2em}}}}
\caption{Bias--variance decomposition of the estimator sequence on the
simulated panel (log scale). Each refinement attacks the component the
previous estimator leaves largest: depth primarily reduces bias here, averaging reduces variance, feature subsampling trades variance
against bias, and regularised boosting holds both down at once.}
\label{fig:sim_ladder}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Is each step a real improvement, or replication noise?}
Table~\ref{tab:sim_ladder} reports averages over the 20 training draws. An
average can hide a lot: is the estimator that looks better on average reliably
better, or are the 20 draws noisy enough that the ranking could flip with
other draws? Every draw refits all six estimators on its own training panel
and scores them on the \emph{same} fixed test panel. The 20 replications
therefore give 20 paired MSE values for each pair of estimators, and a paired
$t$-test on the 20 differences answers the question directly. Writing
$\text{MSE}_A^{(r)}$ and $\text{MSE}_B^{(r)}$ for the two estimators' test MSE
on replication $r$, the test is
\begin{equation}
    H_0: \mathbb{E}\!\left[\text{MSE}_A^{(r)} - \text{MSE}_B^{(r)}\right] = 0,
    \qquad
    H_1: \mathbb{E}\!\left[\text{MSE}_A^{(r)} - \text{MSE}_B^{(r)}\right] \neq 0,
    \label{eq:sim_pairtest}
\end{equation}
This is the same logic as the Diebold--Mariano test used later on the Rossmann
holdout (Equation~\eqref{eq:dm}), but paired over training draws instead of
over time. Rejecting $H_0$ means the average gap in Table~\ref{tab:sim_ladder}
is not just luck in which 20 draws were run. Not rejecting it means the two
estimators cannot be told apart on this panel with this many draws, whatever
their averages say. Because all $\binom{6}{2}=15$ pairs are tested on the same
20 draws, the $p$-values are Holm-corrected, so the stars below control the
family-wise error rate instead of inflating it through repeated testing.

\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\caption{Paired $t$-tests (Equation~\eqref{eq:sim_pairtest}) on the 20
per-replication test MSEs, all $\binom{6}{2}=15$ estimator pairs, ordered by
Holm-adjusted $p$-value. Mean difference is $\overline{\text{MSE}_A-\text{MSE}_B}$
in raw sales$^2$ units. $^{*}p<.05$, $^{**}p<.01$, $^{***}p<.001$ after Holm
correction.}
\label{tab:sim_pairwise}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lrrll}
\toprule
Comparison (A vs B) & Mean diff.\ & $t$ & Holm $p$ & Sig.\ \\
\midrule
<<sim-pairwise-table, results='asis', cache=FALSE>>=
row_lab2 <- c(cart_shallow = "CART (depth 3)", cart_deep = "CART (deep)",
             bagging = "Bagging", rf = "Random Forest",
             xgboost = "XGBoost", lightgbm = "LightGBM")
ft <- full_test[order(full_test$p_holm), ]
for (i in seq_len(nrow(ft))) {
  x <- ft[i, ]
  mn <- function(v, f) sub("^-", "$-$", sprintf(f, v))   # typeset minus
  cat(sprintf("%s vs %s & %s & %s & %s & %s \\\\\n",
              row_lab2[x$A], row_lab2[x$B], mn(x$mean_diff, "%.0f"), mn(x$t, "%.2f"),
              fmt_p_cell(x$p_holm), x$sig))
}
@
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Read against Equation~\eqref{eq:gap_drivers}, the sequence explains why the
empirical comparison in Section~\ref{sec:application} centres on XGBoost and
LightGBM: on this panel they have the lowest estimation error. That is a reason
to use them, but it does not remove tuning or representation effects from the
empirical comparisons. The simulation also shows one caveat that the empirical
results repeat: variance-reduction tricks are not free, and which one wins
depends on how the signal is spread across the features.

\subsection{Statistical Benchmark Models}
\label{sec:statistical}

The tree estimators recover $\hat{\delta}(\mathbf{x}_{s,t})$ from features shared
across stores. The statistical benchmarks take the opposite route. Each is fitted
to a single store's own sales history $\{y_{s,1},\dots,y_{s,T}\}$ with no
cross-store features, so it can estimate the level $\mu_s$ and the part of
$\delta_{s,t}$ that is visible in the series' own autocorrelation, but nothing
that depends on the calendar or promotion variables it never sees. Three are
used, each fitted separately for every store.

\paragraph{Seasonal ARIMA.} For store $s$ the daily series is a seasonal
ARIMA process of order $(p,d,q)(P,D,Q)_7$,
\begin{equation}
    \Phi(L)\,\Phi_7(L^7)\,(1-L)^d (1-L^7)^D\, y_{s,t}
    = \Theta(L)\,\Theta_7(L^7)\, u_{s,t},
    \qquad u_{s,t} \sim \mathrm{WN}(0,\sigma_{u,s}^2),
    \label{eq:arima}
\end{equation}
where $L$ is the lag operator, $\Phi$ and $\Theta$ are the non-seasonal
autoregressive and moving-average polynomials, $\Phi_7$ and $\Theta_7$ their
seasonal (period-7) counterparts, and $u_{s,t}$ a white-noise innovation. This
innovation belongs to the fitted ARIMA model and is not the same object as the
residual in Equation~\eqref{eq:dgp}. The orders $(p,d,q)(P,D,Q)_7$ are chosen per store
by AICc. The differencing $(1-L)^d(1-L^7)^D$ removes trend and weekly seasonality,
so ARIMA recovers the weekly part of $\delta_{s,t}$ through the series' own
autocorrelation, not through a day-of-week feature.

\paragraph{Exponential smoothing (ETS).} An innovations state-space model with
level, trend, and weekly-seasonal states, in additive form,
\begin{align}
    y_{s,t} &= \ell_{t-1} + b_{t-1} + c_{t-7} + u_{s,t}, \label{eq:ets1}\\
    \ell_t &= \ell_{t-1} + b_{t-1} + \alpha_{\ell}\, u_{s,t}, \quad
    b_t = b_{t-1} + \alpha_{b}\, u_{s,t}, \quad
    c_t = c_{t-7} + \alpha_{c}\, u_{s,t}, \label{eq:ets2}
\end{align}
where $\ell_t, b_t, c_t$ are the level, trend, and seasonal states,
$\alpha_{\ell},\alpha_{b},\alpha_{c}$ the corresponding smoothing parameters, and
$u_{s,t}$ the one-step innovation, in the same role as the ARIMA innovation in
Equation~\eqref{eq:arima}. The
error, trend, and seasonal components may each be additive, multiplicative, or
absent, and the form is selected per store by AICc.

\paragraph{Theta.} The Theta method rescales the local curvature of the series.
It forms theta-lines $Z_t(\theta)$ defined by
$\nabla^2 Z_t(\theta) = \theta\, \nabla^2 y_{s,t}$, where $\nabla^2$ is the
second difference, and decomposes the series at $\theta = 0$, a long-run linear
trend, and $\theta = 2$, which doubles the curvature and is extrapolated by
simple exponential smoothing. The forecast averages the two lines
\citep{assimakopoulos2000theta}.

Because all three see only the store's own past, they have no access to the
promotion and calendar variables that carry much of $\delta_{s,t}$.


\paragraph{What each method should be able to reach.}
The univariate benchmarks use each store's full observed history. The
history-only trees of Stage~5 use a few lag summaries that are deliberately
old. Both draw on the store's own past, but they represent it differently and
with different recency, so a flip in the ranking between them does not
identify a hidden demand component. ARIMA, ETS and Theta can pick up recurring
patterns without promotion indicators, but they cannot plan for an upcoming
promotion unless it is given to them as an input. This is a limit of these
particular specifications, not of statistical forecasting in general:
regression with ARIMA errors can take known future covariates
\citep{hyndman2021fpp3}. Section~\ref{sec:arimax_check} runs that comparison on
common stores and windows, and whether the trees still win there is an
empirical question.


\subsection{Dataset Characteristics as Determinants of the Performance Gap}
\label{subsec:chars}

Equation~\eqref{eq:gap_drivers} points to four dataset characteristics that
govern the expected gain over a store-mean baseline.

\paragraph{1. Strength of within-store predictable variation.}
When the predictable swings around the store mean are small, a constant
store-level forecast already captures most of the signal worth capturing. Strong
seasonal or promotional patterns open up more room to improve. M5 carries weekly
seasonality, events, and SNAP cycles at product level. Store-level data retain
day-of-week and promotion effects, but aggregate store totals come out smoother
than product-level series.

\paragraph{2. Feature quality and interaction richness.}
A model can only learn the variation $\mathbf{x}_{s,t}$ actually represents. Lags
supply recent demand information. Promotion, price, and calendar variables
describe what might be driving the changes. The first stage of the empirical
design leaves lags
out and works from calendar, promotion, and store characteristics. Stage~3 then
adds lags and rolling means, so the effect of feature enrichment can be read off
directly.

\paragraph{3. Sample scale: series count and history length.}
Both dimensions on which the Rossmann panel is smaller than M5 enter here.
A global model pools observations across series, and M5's 30490 bottom-level
series against Rossmann's 1115 stores is a difference of more than an order of
magnitude. M5's roughly 5.4 years against Rossmann's 2.5 also leaves fewer
repeated seasonal cycles to estimate from. Both dimensions act on the
estimation-error term of Equation~\eqref{eq:gap_drivers} rather than on the
feature ceiling. More series and longer histories should mean more examples of
recurring calendar or promotion effects and more precise seasonal estimation,
but only where those effects are shared across the panel and where older
observations remain relevant. Predictions~1 and~2 in
Section~\ref{subsec:predictions} state these as testable expectations.

\paragraph{4. Naïve baseline strength.}
Relative improvement depends on the benchmark as much as on the model. A stable
store mean leaves less to improve on. That is why the analysis reports a
store-day-of-week baseline alongside the store mean. It captures weekly
seasonality and sets a tougher bar.

\subsection{Testable Predictions of the Framework}
\label{subsec:predictions}

The framework motivates six testable predictions for the empirical experiments.
They do not follow from the decomposition alone: each also assumes that its
lever moves the quantity it targets, for example that stores share structure a
pooled model can learn.
Each one translates a driver of Equation~\eqref{eq:gap_drivers} into an
\emph{operational} lever that the experiments can move, such as the number of
stores or the store-type composition. The three drivers are the predictable
variation $\mathrm{Var}(\delta_{s,t})$, the feature ceiling $\rho^2_{\infty}$,
and the estimation error. Predictions~1 and~2 move the estimation error,
Predictions~4 and~6 move potential information access, and Prediction~3 changes the composition of
predictable variation. Prediction~5 is the exception: it concerns the
estimators themselves rather than the data, and the simulation in
Section~\ref{sec:estimators} already says something about it.
Section~\ref{sec:reconcile} returns to two cases where the lever turns out to
be a poor proxy for the mechanism it was meant to track.

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*, label=\textbf{Prediction \arabic*.}]
    \item \textbf{Scale.} The ensemble advantage is expected to increase with
    the number of stores because more series provide more opportunities for
    shared pattern learning.

    \item \textbf{Training length.} The ensemble advantage is expected to
    increase with the training window because longer histories provide more
    observations and seasonal repetitions.

    \item \textbf{Heterogeneity.} More homogeneous store groups are expected
    to produce a stronger simple baseline and a smaller ensemble advantage.

    \item \textbf{Feature richness.} Adding lags and rolling means is expected
    to improve XGBoost and LightGBM by supplying recent demand information.

    \item \textbf{Method hierarchy.} LightGBM, XGBoost, and Random Forest are
    expected to perform better than the simpler tree and linear
    specifications because they combine flexible interactions with ensemble
    regularisation. The simulation in Section~\ref{sec:estimators} already
    qualifies the Random Forest half of this claim, since bagging outperforms
    it there once one feature carries most of the signal.

    \item \textbf{Information environment.} As the feature set is enriched from
    a store's own sales history alone to full calendar, store, and promotion
    information, the ensemble advantage should grow. With only its own history a
    boosted model is expected to do no better than the simple and statistical
    baselines, while richer information should make it clearly superior,
    mirroring the shift from the early M competitions to M5.
\end{enumerate}

The fifth prediction concerns the implementations used in this study and is
tested directly by the Stage~1 ranking, where the qualification noted above is
expected to reappear. The sixth prediction concerns the information
environment and is tested by the nested feature experiment in Stage~5.
% ============================================================
\section{Empirical Application: Rossmann Store Sales}
\label{sec:application}
% ============================================================

\subsection{Data}

<<eda-figures, include=FALSE, cache=TRUE>>=
# Regenerates every EDA figure of this section from the raw csv files
