  labs(x = NULL, y = NULL, title = "Correlations among numeric features") +
  theme(axis.text.x = element_text(angle = 45, hjust = 1))
save_fig(p_corr, "fig_eda_corr.pdf", w = 6.5, h = 5.5)
# STL decomposition, calendar-aligned (auto-selects a strong-weekly store)
stl_pick <- df_eda %>% group_by(Store) %>%
  summarise(dow_share = var(ave(Sales, DOW)) / var(Sales), n = n(), .groups = "drop") %>%
  filter(n > 700) %>% slice_max(dow_share, n = 1)
stl_id <- stl_pick$Store
s_stl <- df_eda %>% filter(Store == stl_id) %>% arrange(Date)
full <- tibble(Date = seq(min(s_stl$Date), max(s_stl$Date), by = "day")) %>%
  left_join(s_stl %>% select(Date, Sales), by = "Date")
first_mon <- full$Date[which(wday(full$Date, week_start = 1) == 1)[1]]
full <- full %>% filter(Date >= first_mon)
sfill <- na.approx(full$Sales, na.rm = FALSE)
sfill <- na.locf(na.locf(sfill, na.rm = FALSE), fromLast = TRUE)
ts1 <- ts(sfill, frequency = 7)
dec <- stl(ts1, s.window = "periodic", t.window = 181, robust = TRUE)
pdf("eda_outputs/fig_eda_stl.pdf", width = 8, height = 6)
plot(dec, main = paste("STL decomposition (calendar-aligned), Store", stl_id))
dev.off()
@

\label{sec:data}
% ============================================================

This section describes the data used in the empirical analysis. It introduces
the Rossmann Store Sales dataset and its variables, reports descriptive
statistics and the main empirical patterns, sets out the feature engineering and
the train--test split, contrasts the dataset structurally with M5, and checks the
observed properties against the assumptions behind the model of
Section~\ref{sec:framework}. All figures in this section are produced by
\texttt{rossmann\_eda.Rmd} and exported to \texttt{eda\_outputs/}.

\subsubsection{Overview}
\label{sec:data_overview}

The Rossmann Store Sales dataset was released for a Kaggle competition in 2015
\citep{kaggle2015rossmann}. The task is to forecast daily sales for 1115
Rossmann drugstores from roughly 2.5 years of observations, 1 January 2013 to 31
July 2015. Unlike M5, the target is total daily sales for each store instead of
sales of individual products, so the problem is smaller and more aggregated.

The raw training file records every store--day, including days on which a store
is closed. Removing closed days (\texttt{Open}~$=0$) and the small number of open
days with zero recorded sales leaves the modelling sample of approximately
844{,}000 open store--days used throughout. Closed days are excluded because the
analysis models realised sales on open days. Retaining them would also lower the
store-mean benchmark mechanically and leave the percentage error (MAPE)
undefined.

\paragraph{Review of published competition code.}
The Rossmann competition was won by tree models. \citet{bojer2021kaggle}
review its top solutions: the winner used an ensemble of global XGBoost models,
most other top performers also used ensembles of global XGBoost models, and
the third place was, among the competitions they review, the first time a
neural network reached the top three. The winner did not win through the
algorithm alone. Most of the gain came from features: sales statistics and
their moving averages at different levels, for different days of the week and
promotion periods, counters of the days before, during and after holidays and
promotions, and calendar indicators. This is the
same point this thesis makes with its own experiments, that the trees win
through the information they are given.
The third-place Rossmann solution is public, and its competition-specific
\texttt{kaggle} branch was inspected \citep{guo2015rossmanncode}. Its
\texttt{prepare\_features.py} drops zero-sales training rows, leaves out
\texttt{Customers}, builds promotion and competition-duration features, and
adds outside data on weather, regions and search trends. The README describes
learned category embeddings and averaging over several networks. This is a
useful check on the feature choices made here, but not an accuracy benchmark
to compare against, because the information, the model family and the
validation setup are all different. This thesis keeps a smaller, documented
feature set so that its comparisons stay easy to interpret. Outside data about
the future, such as weather, would also need a check that it is really
available at the forecast origin before it could be used in practice.

\subsubsection{Variables}

The data come in two files. The training file holds daily observations. The
supplementary store file adds time-invariant store characteristics.
Table~\ref{tab:variables} lists the variables used.

\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\caption{Variables in the Rossmann data.}
\label{tab:variables}
\small
\begin{tabular}{ll>{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{6.8cm}}
\toprule
Variable & Type & Description \\
\midrule
Store & Integer & Unique store identifier (1--1115) \\
DayOfWeek & Integer & Day of week (1 = Monday, 7 = Sunday) \\
Date & Date & Calendar date \\
Sales & Integer & Daily turnover in EUR (target variable) \\
Customers & Integer & Number of customers (excluded: unknown at forecast time) \\
Open & Binary & Whether the store was open (0/1) \\
Promo & Binary & Whether a promotion was running (0/1) \\
StateHoliday & Factor & State holiday type (none, public, Easter, Christmas) \\
SchoolHoliday & Binary & Whether schools were on holiday (0/1) \\
\midrule
StoreType & Factor & Store format (A, B, C, D) \\
Assortment & Factor & Assortment level (basic, extra, extended) \\
CompetitionDistance & Numeric & Distance to nearest competitor (metres) \\
CompetitionOpenSince & Date & Date the nearest competitor opened \\
Promo2 & Binary & Participation in a recurring promotion scheme (0/1) \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsubsection{Data Preparation}
\label{sec:cleaning}

Every script in the replication folder applies the same preparation, so that a
result never depends on which file produced it. The steps are few and are set
out here in full.


\paragraph{Row filters.}
The raw file has 1,017,209 rows. Removing the 172,817 closed-day rows leaves
844,392, and removing the 54 open days with zero sales leaves 844,338. The
data do not say why those 54 days are zero. Dropping them gives a clean
target, sales on open days with positive sales, and keeps MAPE defined.

\paragraph{Joining and encoding.}
The store file is joined on \texttt{Store}. The archived tree models code
\texttt{StoreType}, \texttt{Assortment} and \texttt{StateHoliday} as integers.
This gives the categories an order they do not really have: a single split can
only separate, say, types 1--2 from 3--4, not types 1 and 3 from 2 and 4.
Several splits can partly work around this, but with limited depth the coding
still matters, so it is not the same as one-hot or categorical treatment. The
ARIMAX comparison in Section~\ref{sec:arimax_check} uses explicit indicators
instead, including store indicators for the trees.

\paragraph{Missing values.}
Three stores have no \texttt{CompetitionDistance}. The archived scripts fill it
with the median over the joined rows. That is a median weighted by rows, not
the median over stores, and because it is computed before the train--test
split it uses the row mix of later periods, although no test sales enter it.
Filling it from the training data only would be cleaner; the archived results
keep this limitation. The opening date of the nearest competitor is missing
for 354 stores. \texttt{CompetitionOpenMonths} counts the months since that
opening, floored at zero, and stores with an unknown date also get zero. An
unknown date is therefore treated like a competitor that has just opened, not
like one that has always been there. Because every store with a missing date gets the same value, the trees
can partly tell that the date was missing; an explicit missing-value indicator
or a different coding would still be a useful robustness check.

\paragraph{Calendar alignment.}
The main tree analyses keep only open days with positive sales, so their lag
operators count retained rows, not calendar days. The statistical benchmarks
need a regular daily calendar, so for them closed days are filled with zero.
The ARIMAX comparison trains every model on the same daily calendar and scores
them on the same open days with positive sales.


\paragraph{What is not done.} No sales value is trimmed, winsorised or
transformed. This is a decision, not an oversight: the distribution has a
long right tail, as the skewness in Table~\ref{tab:descstats} shows, and the
largest days sit far above the median.

Those days are kept because they are the phenomenon under study. A sales figure
several times a store's usual level is typically a promotion, a pre-holiday
surge, or the reopening after a closure, and all three are exactly the
predictable within-store variation $\delta_{s,t}$ that the thesis asks whether a
model can recover. Removing them would remove the signal and leave an easier
problem on which every method looks better than it is. A trimmed sample would
also flatter the store-mean baseline, which is most exposed to the tail, and so
would narrow the very gap being measured. Dropping the 54 zero-sales open days
is a choice about the target, not a claim that those days are recording
errors; the data cannot tell.

The raw euro scale is kept for a related reason: RMSE is then read in the units
of the quantity being forecast, and a log transform would change what the loss
function penalises, weighting proportional errors on small stores as heavily as
absolute errors on large ones.

The derived calendar and store features are listed separately in
Section~\ref{sec:features}, and \texttt{Customers} is excluded from every model
for the reason given there.

\subsubsection{Descriptive Statistics}

Table~\ref{tab:descstats} summarises the distribution of the target across all
open trading days. Daily store sales average about 6960 euros, with a standard
deviation near 3100 and a pronounced right skew.

\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\caption{Descriptive statistics: daily store sales (EUR), open days only.}
\label{tab:descstats}
\begin{tabular}{lr}
\toprule
Statistic & Value \\
\midrule
<<tab-descstats, results='asis', cache=FALSE>>=
d <- tr_read("descriptive_stats.csv")     # rossmann_eda.Rmd
if (is.null(d)) {
  tt_missing(2, "descriptive_stats.csv", "rossmann_eda.Rmd")
} else {
  pretty <- c("Std Dev" = "Std.\\ Dev.\\ ")
  for (i in seq_len(nrow(d))) {
    x <- d[i, ]
    lab <- if (x$Statistic %in% names(pretty)) pretty[[x$Statistic]] else x$Statistic
    # Skewness is unitless and reported to two decimals; the rest to one.
    v <- if (identical(x$Statistic, "Skewness")) tt_num(x$Value, 2) else tt_num(x$Value, 1)
    cat(sprintf("%s & %s \\\\\n", lab, v))
  }
}
@
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The right skew in the raw target (Figure~\ref{fig:dist}, raw panel) means that
a few large errors weigh heavily in RMSE. The skew by itself does not favour
any forecasting family. Trees make no assumption about the distribution of the
target, and the log panel is there only to make the skew visible.

