\subsubsection{Feature Engineering}
\label{sec:features}

Raw date and store fields are turned into a set of model-ready features used by
all tree-based models, listed in Table~\ref{tab:features}. The variable
\texttt{Customers} is excluded from every model because it is not available at
forecast time. A practitioner predicting tomorrow's sales does not yet know how
many customers will visit, so including it would constitute data leakage.

\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\caption{Engineered features used in all tree-based models.}
\label{tab:features}
\small
\begin{tabular}{ll}
\toprule
Feature & Description \\
\midrule
Store & Store ID (numeric) \\
DayOfWeek & Day of week (1--7) \\
Promo & Promotion active (0/1) \\
StateHoliday & State holiday type coded 0--3 (none, public, Easter, Christmas) \\
SchoolHoliday & School holiday indicator (0/1) \\
StoreType & Store format encoded as integer (1--4) \\
Assortment & Assortment level encoded as integer (1--3) \\
CompetitionDistance & Distance to nearest competitor (metres) \\
CompetitionOpenMonths & Months since nearest competitor opened \\
Promo2 & Participating in recurring promo scheme (0/1) \\
Year, Month, Week & Calendar year, month (1--12), ISO week (1--53) \\
DayOfMonth, Quarter & Day of month (1--31), calendar quarter (1--4) \\
IsWeekend & Saturday or Sunday indicator (0/1) \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsubsection{Train--Test Split}
\label{sec:split}

All models are evaluated on a temporal hold-out: the final six weeks (42 days),
from 20 June to 31 July 2015, form the test set, and all earlier observations
form the training set, a span chosen because six complete weeks give every day
of the week equal representation. A temporal split mirrors real forecasting
practice and is necessary to avoid leaking future information into training. A random split would
let a model interpolate within the test period rather than extrapolate beyond the
training data. Stages~1--3 and~5 keep this test period fixed to isolate changes
in training conditions and features, while Stage~4 moves the forecast origin to
check robustness across windows.

\subsubsection{Comparison with the M5 Dataset}

Several structural differences between Rossmann and M5 bear directly on the
model of Section~\ref{sec:framework} and motivate using Rossmann as an external
test of the M5 result. Table~\ref{tab:m5compare} summarises them.

\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\caption{Structural comparison of the Rossmann and M5 datasets.}
\label{tab:m5compare}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\toprule
Property & Rossmann & M5 (Walmart) \\
\midrule
Country & Germany & United States \\
Retailer type & Drugstore chain & General merchandise \\
Bottom-level series & 1115 stores & 30490 product--store \\
Target & Aggregate store sales (EUR) & Units per product--store \\
History & $\approx$2.5 years & $\approx$5.4 years \\
Frequency & Daily & Daily \\
Intermittency & Low (aggregated) & High (product level) \\
Prices & Not observed & Product-level prices \\
Calendar/promotion & Promotions, holidays & Events, SNAP, prices \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The differences pull in a consistent direction. Rossmann offers a global model
far fewer related series, a shorter history with fewer seasonal repetitions, and
no product-level prices, while its aggregation makes sales smoother and less
intermittent, which should strengthen the simple store-level baselines. If
LightGBM's M5 performance leaned partly on the scale and feature richness of that
dataset, its advantage need not survive here.

\subsubsection{Properties versus Model Assumptions}

The framework treats each store's level as roughly stable within an evaluation
window. The exploratory plots show recurring weekly and promotion patterns, but
also trends and level shifts that strain this assumption.
Table~\ref{tab:storetypes} reports the average within-store weekly share on
open trading days: 0.29 for Type~A and 0.39 for Type~B. These are descriptive
numbers over the full sample, not training-only estimates of all predictable
variation. A pooled variance ratio that also counts differences between store
levels would answer a different question, so it is not used in their place.
The calendar-aligned STL plot in Figure~\ref{fig:stl} is only an
illustration: its interpolated closed days and smoothing choices mean it cannot
identify the hidden components of Equation~\eqref{eq:dgp}.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\IfFileExists{eda_outputs/fig_eda_stl.pdf}{\includegraphics[width=0.60\textwidth]{fig_eda_stl.pdf}}{\fbox{\parbox{0.92\textwidth}{\centering\vspace{2em}[fig\_eda\_stl]\vspace{2em}}}}
\caption{Calendar-aligned STL decomposition of one store's daily series into
trend, weekly seasonal, and remainder components. Closed days are filled by
linear interpolation so the weekly cycle stays aligned to actual days of the week.}
\label{fig:stl}
\end{figure}

\FloatBarrier

% Transition into research design
The next subsection sets out the experiments.

\subsection{Research Design}
\label{sec:design}
% ============================================================

\subsubsection{Overview}

The empirical analysis has five main stages, followed by a set of additional
analyses (Stage~6, Section~\ref{sec:additional}) that probe the internal validity
of the results. Section~\ref{sec:arimax_check} adds one more check: it gives
ARIMAX the same covariates as the trees, to see whether the tree advantage
comes from the extra information rather than from the trees themselves.

\textbf{Stage 1: Full Rossmann comparison.} Eleven specifications meet on the
final 42-day holdout: a store-mean baseline, a store-day-of-week baseline,
Auto-ARIMA, ETS, Theta, linear regression, CART, bagging, Random Forest,
XGBoost, and LightGBM. This stage fixes the initial ranking without lag features.

\textbf{Stage 2: Subsetting experiments.} The analysis changes the number of
stores, the length of the training window, and the store-type composition.
XGBoost, LightGBM, and both simple baselines are evaluated in each condition.
This stage tests the scale, training-length, and heterogeneity expectations.

\textbf{Stage 3: Feature enrichment.} Sales lags and rolling means are added,
after which XGBoost and LightGBM are re-estimated on the full dataset. The
comparison shows how much recent sales information changes their performance.

\textbf{Stage 4: Rolling test-window robustness.} The no-lag comparison is
repeated over four 42-day periods. This checks whether the
main XGBoost--LightGBM ranking depends on the final holdout window
\citep{ma2022robustness}.

\textbf{Stage 5: Incremental information sets.} XGBoost and LightGBM are
re-estimated on four nested feature sets, starting from a store's own sales
history and adding calendar, store, and promotion information in turn. All
features are available at the forecast origin, so this stage provides a
fixed-origin counterpart to Stage~3 and tests the information-environment
explanation developed in the literature review.

\textbf{Stage 6: Additional analyses.} A set of robustness and internal-validity
checks on the main results: fixed-origin recursive lag forecasts,
Diebold--Mariano significance tests, replication standard errors for the
number-of-stores experiment, a direct store-level test of the framework, a
hyperparameter-capacity grid, and the fixed-composition and global-versus-local
comparisons. These do not introduce new conditions. They probe how robust the
first five stages are.

The stages are operational designs, but each is included because it targets a
data property from the framework. Table~\ref{tab:lever_properties} makes this
mapping explicit. The aim is to avoid interpreting a Rossmann-specific lever,
such as the number of stores, as though it were the general principle itself.

\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\caption{Experimental levers and the data properties they target.}
\label{tab:lever_properties}
\small
\begin{tabular}{>{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{3.1cm}
                >{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{4.0cm}
                >{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{5.8cm}}
\toprule
Experiment & Operational variation & Data property targeted \\
\midrule
Stage 1 & Full-sample model ranking & Whether flexible estimators recover predictable structure beyond simple baselines \\
Stage 2, \newline store count & 50, 200, 500, and 1115 stores & Cross-series learning potential and composition-induced heterogeneity \\
Stage 2, \newline training length & 6, 12, 18, 24 months, and full history & Seasonal repetition and estimation precision \\
Stage 2, \newline store type & A, B, C+D, and full sample & Baseline strength and predictable within-store variation \\
Stage 3 & No lags versus lag and rolling features & Recoverability of recent demand structure by the feature set \\
Stage 4 & Four 42-day test windows & Dependence of the measured advantage on the forecast origin \\
Stage 5 & History, calendar, store, and promotion feature sets & Observable signal richness, corresponding to the feature ceiling $\rho^2_\infty$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsubsection{Models}

Eleven specifications meet on the Stage~1 holdout. Their mechanics are set out
in Sections~\ref{sec:estimators} (tree-based) and~\ref{sec:statistical}
(statistical). Table~\ref{tab:specs} records only the estimation settings used
here.

\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\caption{Stage~1 specifications and their estimation settings. Mechanics are
described in Section~\ref{sec:estimators} for the tree-based estimators and
Section~\ref{sec:statistical} for the statistical benchmarks. Early stopping
uses the last 15\% of training dates as a temporal validation slice.}
\label{tab:specs}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lp{9.4cm}}
\toprule
Specification & Estimation settings \\
\midrule
Store-mean & $\hat{y}_{s,t} = \bar{y}_s$, mean open-day training sales per store \\
Store-day-of-week & mean training sales per store $\times$ day of week \\
Auto-ARIMA & Equation~\eqref{eq:arima}, per store via \texttt{forecast}; orders by AICc, stepwise approximate search on the weekly-frequency series \\
ETS & Equations~\eqref{eq:ets1}--\eqref{eq:ets2}, per store via \texttt{forecast::ets}; error, trend, seasonal forms by AICc \\
Theta & Section~\ref{sec:statistical}, per store via \texttt{forecast::thetaf} \\
Linear regression & OLS on the numeric Stage~1 features, store ID included as a number; a simple reference, not a well-specified linear model \\
CART & \texttt{rpart}; \texttt{maxdepth} 20, \texttt{cp} 0.00001, \texttt{minsplit} 10 \\
Bagging & \texttt{ranger}; $M = 200$ deep trees, \texttt{mtry} $= p$ (every split may use all predictors) \\
Random Forest & \texttt{ranger}; $M = 200$ deep trees, \texttt{mtry} $\approx p/3$ (regression default) \\
XGBoost & \texttt{xgboost}; max depth 6, $\eta = 0.10$, rounds by early stopping, subsample 0.8, colsample 0.8 \\
LightGBM & \texttt{lightgbm}; \texttt{num\_leaves} $= 63$, $\eta = 0.05$, rounds by early stopping, subsample 0.8, colsample 0.8 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Bagging and the Random Forest differ only in \texttt{mtry}. LightGBM's
leaf-wise 63-leaf growth is a different capacity profile from the depth-6
XGBoost model.

For ARIMA, ETS, and Theta, closed days are retained as zero-sales observations
when constructing each daily series so that weekly spacing is not distorted.
A closed day is not literally a zero-sales day, so this could in principle bias
the level and weekly seasonal of these models downward. A robustness check that
instead fills closed days by interpolation does not bear this out. It leaves
Auto-ARIMA and ETS slightly worse (by about 2\% and 8\%) and improves only Theta
(by about 11\%), so the zero-fill is not what places the statistical benchmarks
behind the boosted trees. Their accuracy is in any case evaluated only on the
open-store test observations used for all other models.

The models are run at standard configurations instead of each being grid-tuned
to its best. The tree depths differ by design, since the two boosted models add
many trees and so use shallow ones (depth six and 63 leaves) with the number of
rounds chosen by early stopping on a temporal validation slice, while CART,
bagging, and the random forest average or stand on deep trees. The comparison is
therefore between sensible default specifications, and Stage~6 probes the
sensitivity of the boosted models to their capacity settings directly.

\subsubsection{Evaluation Metrics}

RMSE is the primary ranking metric, while MAE and MAPE provide additional
views of forecast error. M5 used WRMSSE, which scales and weights
product-level series within a hierarchy. Rossmann contains aggregate store
sales and does not have the same hierarchy. Numerical improvements in M5 and
Rossmann are therefore not directly comparable. The relevant comparison is
whether the broad model ordering carries over.

All metrics are computed over $\mathcal{S}$, the set of test-period store--day
pairs, with $n = |\mathcal{S}|$, $y_{s,t}$ observed sales as in
Equation~\eqref{eq:dgp}, and $\hat{y}_{s,t}$ the model's prediction:
\begin{equation}
    \text{RMSE} = \sqrt{\frac{1}{n} \sum_{(s,t)\in\mathcal{S}} \left(y_{s,t} - \hat{y}_{s,t}\right)^2},
    \qquad
    \text{MAE} = \frac{1}{n} \sum_{(s,t)\in\mathcal{S}} \left|y_{s,t} - \hat{y}_{s,t}\right|,
    \label{eq:rmse}
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
    \text{MAPE} = \frac{100\%}{n} \sum_{(s,t)\in\mathcal{S}}
    \left|\frac{y_{s,t} - \hat{y}_{s,t}}{y_{s,t}}\right|,
    \qquad
    \Delta_{\text{rel}} = \frac{\text{RMSE}^{\text{naive}} - \text{RMSE}^{\text{model}}}
    {\text{RMSE}^{\text{naive}}} \times 100\%.
    \label{eq:improvement}
\end{equation}
RMSE, in euros, weights large errors most heavily and is the ranking metric.
MAPE is undefined when $y_{s,t}=0$, a further reason closed-store observations
are excluded. Positive $\Delta_{\text{rel}}$ means lower RMSE than the
store-mean baseline. Improvement over Store-DOW is also reported for the main
ensemble models.

\subsubsection{Stage 2: Subsetting Experiment Design}
\label{sec:subsets}

The subsetting experiments vary one aspect of the Rossmann sample while
retaining the final 42-day test period. XGBoost, LightGBM, Store-mean, and
Store-DOW are recalculated for each condition.

\paragraph{Dimension 1: Number of stores.}
Stores are sampled without replacement to create subsets of
$N\in\{50,200,500,1115\}$. The first three conditions use 20 random draws. The full condition uses all stores once. Averaging across draws reduces the
influence of any particular small-store sample.

\paragraph{Dimension 2: Training window length.}
The training sample retains the most recent 6, 12, 18, or 24 months, or the
full available history. The test period remains unchanged.

\paragraph{Dimension 3: Store type composition.}
The store-type conditions are A alone, B alone, the balanced C$+$D mix, and the
full sample. The C$+$D mix replaces a Type-A-plus-B grouping that would have been
dominated by Type~A, since Type~B has only 17 stores. Type~B is still reported
on its own but interpreted cautiously for the same reason.

\subsubsection{Stage 3: Feature Enrichment (Phase 2)}
\label{sec:phase2}

Stage~3 adds sales lags and rolling means to XGBoost and LightGBM. For store
$s$ on day $t$, the lag variables are:

\begin{equation}
    \text{lag}_k(s,t) = y_{s,\, t - k}, \quad k \in \{1, 7, 14, 28\}
    \label{eq:lags}
\end{equation}

Here, $k=1$ denotes the previous retained trading observation and $k=7$ seven
retained trading observations earlier.
The rolling means are:

\begin{align}
    \text{roll}_{7}(s,t) &= \frac{1}{7} \sum_{k=1}^{7} y_{s,\, t-k}
    \label{eq:roll7} \\
    \text{roll}_{28}(s,t) &= \frac{1}{28} \sum_{k=1}^{28} y_{s,\, t-k}
    \label{eq:roll28}
\end{align}

Here $t$ counts retained trading days, not calendar days, so seven or 28 steps
back is usually a little more than one or four calendar weeks. The rolling
means use only earlier observations. During the holdout, lag variables
are updated as actual sales become available. Stage~3 is therefore a rolling
one-day-ahead evaluation, not a fixed-origin 42-day forecast.

The comparison tests whether recent sales information improves the two
boosted models and whether it narrows their Stage~1 performance gap.

\subsubsection{Stage 4: Rolling Test-Window Robustness}
\label{sec:rolling}

The no-lag comparison is repeated for four 42-day windows. For each window,
training uses all observations before the first test date.
Store-mean, Store-DOW, XGBoost, and LightGBM are then re-estimated.

Using the no-lag feature set keeps test-period sensitivity separate from the
lag experiment. The ranking is considered stable across these windows if the
same boosted model has lower RMSE in each period.

\subsubsection{Stage 5: Incremental Information Sets}
\label{sec:incremental_design}

The literature review argued that the change in machine-learning performance
between the early M competitions and M5 reflects the information environment
as much as the algorithms. Stage~5 turns this argument into an experiment
within Rossmann. XGBoost and LightGBM are re-estimated on four nested feature
sets that move step by step from an old-M-style setting to the full Rossmann
feature set:

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
    \item \textbf{History only:} store identity plus origin-safe summaries of
    the store's own sales history (lags of 42, 49, 56, and 84 retained trading observations and
    pre-origin rolling means over 7 and 28 days; 7 features). This
    approximates the univariate information available to participants in the
    early M competitions.
    \item \textbf{$+$ Calendar:} day-of-week, month, week, day-of-month,
    quarter, weekend indicator, year, and a trend term (15 features).
    \item \textbf{$+$ Store attributes:} store type, assortment, competitor
    distance, and competition history (19 features).
    \item \textbf{$+$ Promotions and holidays:} promotion indicators, Promo2,
    and state and school holidays (23 features).
\end{enumerate}

The stores, training period, 42-day test period, model settings, and metrics
are identical across the four sets, so performance changes can be attributed
to the added information. Unlike Stage~3, no realised sales from the test
period enter any predictor. The shortest lag is 42 retained rows, and a 42-day
holdout has at most 42 retained rows per store, so every lag value is already
known at the origin. These values can be much older than the latest
observation ARIMA uses, so this comparison depends on recency and on how the
history is represented, not only on the model family. Store-mean and Store-DOW
are recomputed inside the experiment as fixed reference points. The warm-up
rows dropped to build the origin-safe features lower them slightly compared
with Stage~1 (Store-DOW 1647 against 1665).

% ============================================================
\subsection{Results}
\label{sec:results}
% ============================================================

The results follow the five stages of the research design, then the
covariate-matched ARIMAX check and the additional Stage~6 analyses.
$\Delta_{\text{rel}}$ is the improvement over Store-mean and
$\Delta_{\text{DOW}}$ the improvement over the stronger Store-DOW benchmark.
The stages follow the framework; they are not a set of unrelated model
comparisons. Stage~1 asks whether the boosted estimators recover more
predictable structure than the baselines on the full Rossmann sample.
Stages~2--5 then change empirical counterparts of $\mathrm{Var}(\delta)$ and
$\rho^2_\infty$: the amount of recurring structure, the history available to
estimate it, the store mix and the information in the feature set.
Section~\ref{sec:properties} finally replaces the Rossmann stage labels with
store-level data properties, so the answer to the research question is not
tied only to the number of stores or months in this particular dataset.

% ------------------------------------------------------------
\subsubsection{Stage 1: Full-Dataset Baseline Comparison}
% ------------------------------------------------------------

Table~\ref{tab:stage1} reports the full-sample comparison without lag
features, ordered by RMSE.

\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\caption{Stage~1 results: full Rossmann dataset, no lag features.
$\Delta_{\text{rel}}$ is defined as $(RMSE_{\text{na\"{i}ve}} - RMSE_{\text{model}}) /
RMSE_{\text{na\"{i}ve}} \times 100\%$; positive values indicate improvement over the
store-mean na\"{i}ve baseline. $\Delta_{\text{DOW}}$ reports improvement over the
store-day-of-week na\"{i}ve baseline.}
\label{tab:stage1}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lrrrrr}
\toprule
Model          & RMSE    & MAE     & MAPE (\%) & $\Delta_{\text{rel}}$ (\%) & $\Delta_{\text{DOW}}$ (\%) \\
\midrule
<<tab-stage1, results='asis', cache=FALSE>>=
# Table 11 is assembled from three scripts, because the pooled models, the
# random forest at mtry = p/3, and the per-store statistical benchmarks are
# fitted in three different files. See README.md section 5.
pooled <- tr_read("stage1_pooled_models.csv")      # rossmann_stage1_models.Rmd
rfcfg  <- tr_read("bagging_rf_configs.csv")        # rossmann_bagging_only.Rmd
statb  <- tr_read("statistical_benchmarks.csv")    # rossmann_old_m_to_modern_ml_experiment.Rmd

if (is.null(pooled)) {
  tt_missing(6, "stage1_pooled_models.csv", "rossmann_stage1_models.Rmd")
} else {
  # Pretty names, and the subset of models the thesis reports.
  nm <- c("XGBoost" = "XGBoost", "LightGBM" = "LightGBM", "Bagging" = "Bagging",
          "CART" = "CART", "Linear" = "Linear",
          "Store-DOW naive" = "Store-DOW na\\\"{i}ve",
          "Store-mean naive" = "Store-mean na\\\"{i}ve")
  rows <- pooled %>% filter(Model %in% names(nm)) %>%
    transmute(Label = nm[Model], RMSE, MAE, MAPE, Delta_rel, Delta_DOW)

  # Random forest: the thesis reports the regression setting mtry ~ p/3, which
  # rossmann_stage1_models.Rmd does not fit (it uses the ranger default).
  if (!is.null(rfcfg)) {
    r <- rfcfg %>% filter(grepl("p/3", Model, fixed = TRUE))
    if (nrow(r) == 1)
      rows <- bind_rows(rows, tibble(
        Label = "Random Forest", RMSE = r$RMSE[1], MAE = r$MAE[1],
        MAPE = r$MAPE[1], Delta_rel = r$delta_mean[1], Delta_DOW = r$delta_dow[1]))
  }

  # Statistical benchmarks, fitted per store.
  if (!is.null(statb)) {
    sn <- c("ARIMA" = "Auto-ARIMA", "Theta" = "Theta", "ETS" = "ETS")
    ref_mean <- rows$RMSE[rows$Label == "Store-mean na\\\"{i}ve"]
    ref_dow  <- rows$RMSE[rows$Label == "Store-DOW na\\\"{i}ve"]
    s <- statb %>% filter(Model %in% names(sn)) %>%
      transmute(Label = sn[Model], RMSE, MAE, MAPE,
                Delta_rel = (ref_mean - RMSE) / ref_mean * 100,
                Delta_DOW = (ref_dow  - RMSE) / ref_dow  * 100)
    rows <- bind_rows(rows, s)
  }

  # The two baselines have no improvement over themselves.
  rows$Delta_rel[rows$Label == "Store-mean na\\\"{i}ve"] <- NA
  rows$Delta_DOW[rows$Label == "Store-mean na\\\"{i}ve"] <- NA
  rows$Delta_DOW[rows$Label == "Store-DOW na\\\"{i}ve"]  <- NA

  rows <- rows %>% arrange(RMSE)
  for (i in seq_len(nrow(rows))) {
    x <- rows[i, ]
    cat(sprintf("%s & %.0f & %.0f & %s & %s & %s \\\\\n",
                x$Label, x$RMSE, x$MAE, tt_num(x$MAPE, 1),
                tt_signed(x$Delta_rel), tt_signed(x$Delta_DOW)))
  }
}
@
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}


<<stage1-figure, include=FALSE, cache=FALSE>>=
# Draws the result figures (Stages 1-5, property coefficients, framework test)
# from the same CSVs as their tables and saves them to rossmann_outputs/ and
# eda_outputs/, where the \includegraphics calls below pick them up.
# Previously in scripts/result_figures.R; kept here so the thesis needs no
# separate script file.
dir.create("rossmann_outputs", showWarnings = FALSE)
dir.create("eda_outputs", showWarnings = FALSE)
plot_save <- function(p, name, w = 8, h = 4) {
  ggsave(paste0("rossmann_outputs/", name, ".pdf"), p, width = w, height = h)
  ggsave(paste0("rossmann_outputs/", name, ".png"), p, width = w, height = h, dpi = 160)
}
pal_m <- c("Store-DOW" = "#777777", "XGBoost" = "#4477AA",
           "LightGBM" = "#CC6677", "Store-mean" = "#AA4499")

# Stage 1: test RMSE of every model, ordered.
p <- tr_read("stage1_pooled_models.csv")
p <- p %>% filter(!Model %in% c("Random Forest", "GBM (scratch)")) %>% select(Model, RMSE)
r <- tr_read("bagging_rf_configs.csv") %>% filter(grepl("p/3", Model, fixed = TRUE))
p <- bind_rows(p, tibble(Model = "Random Forest", RMSE = r$RMSE),
               tr_read("statistical_benchmarks.csv") %>%
                 filter(Model %in% c("ARIMA", "ETS", "Theta")) %>% select(Model, RMSE))
p$Model <- recode(p$Model, "ARIMA" = "Auto-ARIMA", "Store-DOW naive" = "Store-DOW",
                  "Store-mean naive" = "Store-mean")
g <- ggplot(p, aes(RMSE, forcats::fct_reorder(Model, RMSE))) + geom_col(fill = "#4477AA") +
  geom_text(aes(label = round(RMSE)), hjust = -.12, size = 3) +
  geom_vline(xintercept = p$RMSE[p$Model == "Store-DOW"], linetype = 2) +
  scale_x_continuous(expand = expansion(mult = c(0, .14))) +
  labs(x = "Test RMSE (EUR)", y = NULL) + theme_minimal(base_size = 11)
plot_save(g, "fig_stage1_rmse", 8, 5)

# Stage 2: gain over Store-mean by number of stores.
d <- tr_read("stage2_nstores.csv") %>% select(N, StoreDOW_delta, XGB_delta, LGBM_delta) %>%
  pivot_longer(-N, names_to = "Model", values_to = "Gain")
d$Model <- recode(d$Model, StoreDOW_delta = "Store-DOW", XGB_delta = "XGBoost", LGBM_delta = "LightGBM")
g <- ggplot(d, aes(factor(N), Gain, color = Model, group = Model)) + geom_line() + geom_point() +
  scale_color_manual(values = pal_m) +
  labs(x = "Number of stores", y = "Improvement over Store-mean (%)") +
  theme_minimal() + theme(legend.position = "top")
plot_save(g, "fig_stage2_nstores_gain")

# Stage 2: gain over Store-mean by training window.
d <- tr_read("stage2_window.csv"); d$Window <- factor(d$Window, levels = d$Window)
d <- d %>% pivot_longer(-Window, names_to = "Model", values_to = "Gain")
d$Model <- recode(d$Model, StoreDOW_delta = "Store-DOW", XGB_delta = "XGBoost", LGBM_delta = "LightGBM")
g <- ggplot(d, aes(Window, Gain, color = Model, group = Model)) + geom_line() + geom_point() +
  scale_color_manual(values = pal_m) +
  labs(x = NULL, y = "Improvement over Store-mean (%)") +
  theme_minimal() + theme(legend.position = "top")
plot_save(g, "fig_stage2_window_gain")

# Stage 3: RMSE without and with updated lags.
d <- bind_rows(
  tr_read("stage1_pooled_models.csv") %>%
    filter(Model %in% c("XGBoost", "LightGBM", "Store-DOW naive", "Store-mean naive")) %>%
    select(Model, RMSE) %>% mutate(Set = "Fixed origin, no lags"),
  tr_read("stage3_lags.csv") %>% select(Model, RMSE) %>% mutate(Set = "Updated one-step lags"))
d$Model <- recode(d$Model, "Store-DOW naive" = "Store-DOW", "Store-mean naive" = "Store-mean")
g <- ggplot(d, aes(Model, RMSE, fill = Set)) + geom_col(position = "dodge") +
  geom_text(aes(label = round(RMSE)), position = position_dodge(.9), vjust = -.3, size = 3) +
  scale_fill_manual(values = c("#888888", "#4477AA")) +
  scale_y_continuous(expand = expansion(mult = c(0, .12))) +
  labs(x = NULL, y = "RMSE (EUR)") + theme_minimal() + theme(legend.position = "top")
plot_save(g, "fig_stage3_rmse")

# Stage 4: rolling test windows, plus the December window.
d  <- tr_read("stage4_rolling.csv") %>% select(Window, Store_mean, Store_DOW, XGBoost, LightGBM)
de <- tail(tr_read("rolling_december.csv"), 1) %>%
  select(Window, Store_mean, Store_DOW, XGBoost, LightGBM)
de$Window <- "Window 4 (Christmas)"
d <- bind_rows(d, de) %>% pivot_longer(-Window, names_to = "Model", values_to = "RMSE")
d$Model <- recode(d$Model, Store_mean = "Store-mean", Store_DOW = "Store-DOW")
g <- ggplot(d, aes(Window, RMSE, color = Model, group = Model)) + geom_line() + geom_point() +
  scale_color_manual(values = pal_m) + labs(x = NULL, y = "Test RMSE (EUR)") +
  theme_minimal() + theme(legend.position = "top")
plot_save(g, "fig_rolling_window_robustness")

# Stage 5: RMSE along the nested information sets.
d <- tr_read("stage5_information_sets.csv"); d$Step <- as.integer(substr(d[[1]], 1, 1))
ref <- median(d$RMSE / (1 - d$Delta_DOW / 100))
g <- ggplot(d, aes(Step, RMSE, color = Model)) + geom_line() + geom_point() +
  geom_text(aes(label = round(RMSE)), vjust = -.6, size = 3) +
  geom_hline(yintercept = ref, linetype = 2, color = "grey40") +
  scale_color_manual(values = pal_m) +
  scale_x_continuous(breaks = 1:4, labels = c("History", "+ Calendar", "+ Store", "+ Commercial")) +
  labs(x = NULL, y = "Fixed-origin RMSE (EUR)",
       caption = "Dashed line: Store-DOW, reconstructed from the reported gains (rounding applies).") +
  theme_minimal() + theme(legend.position = "top")
plot_save(g, "fig_stage5_information_path")

# Data-property regression coefficients.
d <- tr_read("gain_vs_properties.csv")
g <- ggplot(d, aes(Coefficient, forcats::fct_reorder(Property, Coefficient))) +
  geom_col(fill = "#4477AA") + geom_vline(xintercept = 0) +
  labs(x = "Coefficient (percentage points per predictor SD)", y = NULL) + theme_minimal()
ggsave("eda_outputs/fig_gain_vs_properties.pdf", g, width = 7, height = 4)

# Store-level framework test.
d <- read.csv(file.path(TT_DIR, "tab_e4_store_level_framework.csv"))
d$gain_mse <- d$rmse_dow^2 - d$rmse_lgbm^2
g <- ggplot(d, aes(var_explained / 1e6, gain_mse / 1e6)) + geom_point(alpha = .35, size = 1) +
  geom_smooth(method = "lm", formula = y ~ x, se = FALSE, color = "#4477AA") +
  labs(x = "Predictable-variation proxy (million EUR squared)",
       y = "Store-DOW minus LightGBM MSE (million EUR squared)",
       caption = sprintf("Pearson correlation: %.2f; n = %d stores.",
                         cor(d$var_explained, d$gain_mse), nrow(d))) + theme_minimal()
plot_save(g, "fig_framework_test", 7, 4.5)
@


\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.72\textwidth]{fig_stage1_rmse}
\caption{Stage~1 RMSE comparison on the full Rossmann dataset without lag
features, ordered by RMSE. The dashed line marks the Store-DOW baseline.}
\label{fig:stage1_rmse}
\end{figure}

XGBoost, LightGBM, bagging, and Random Forest all outperform both baselines, but
the baseline comparison is itself informative. Store-DOW reduces RMSE from 1934
to 1665, showing that weekly seasonality accounts for a meaningful part of the
forecast error before any flexible model is introduced. Relative to this stronger
benchmark, XGBoost improves RMSE by \Sexpr{tr_dow("XGBoost")}\%, LightGBM by
\Sexpr{tr_dow("LightGBM")}\%, and Random Forest by
\Sexpr{tr_dow("RandomForest")}\%, so the ensemble advantage cannot be attributed only to a weak
store-mean comparison. The trees also recover predictable structure beyond the
weekly pattern.

One ordering inside the tree family stands out. Bagging and the random
forest are the same bootstrap-averaged ensemble at two feature-subsampling rates,
bagging letting every split use all $p$ predictors and the random forest a random
subset of $p^{*} \approx p/3$. On this data the gain over the store mean is larger
when every feature is available at each split, so bagging (RMSE
\Sexpr{tr_rmse("Bagging")}) sits just behind the two boosted models and a
little ahead of the random forest (\Sexpr{tr_rmse("RandomForest")}).
With the forecast origin fixed at the final 42 days, letting each split see
only a few features hides part of the calendar structure the test window
depends on. Feature subsampling usually helps by decorrelating the trees, but
here it costs more than it saves. This is the same trade-off the simulation in
Section~\ref{sec:estimators} shows: subsampling lowered the correlation
$\hat{\kappa}$ between trees but raised the bias enough to leave the random
forest behind bagging (Table~\ref{tab:sim_ladder}). Whether subsampling pays
off depends on the data, a point the scale and global--local results come
back to.

\paragraph{Comparison with Dedicated Statistical Models.}

All three statistical models were fitted separately for each of the 1115 stores
and evaluated on the same holdout observations as the Stage~1 models, and no
store-level fit failed. Auto-ARIMA is the strongest of the three, reaching an
RMSE of \Sexpr{tr_rmse("ARIMA")} and a \Sexpr{tr_dow("ARIMA")}\% improvement
over Store-DOW, while Theta records \Sexpr{tr_rmse("Theta")} and a
\Sexpr{tr_dow("Theta")}\% improvement and ETS reaches \Sexpr{tr_rmse("ETS")},
which is worse than
Store-DOW although still better than Store-mean. These are real forecasting
models rather than elementary averages, so they raise the bar considerably. Both
boosted models still clear it. XGBoost and LightGBM outperform all three on the
primary holdout, and even the Random Forest beats Auto-ARIMA by a wide margin
(\Sexpr{tr_rmse("RandomForest")} against \Sexpr{tr_rmse("ARIMA")}). The strength of the ARIMA result matters
for the argument all the same, because it shows that the ensemble advantage is
not automatic. A well-specified store-level statistical model still beats the
unpenalised linear regression and comes close to a single CART tree.

Within the boosted pair the margin is much narrower. XGBoost records an RMSE of
\Sexpr{tr_rmse("XGBoost")} against LightGBM's \Sexpr{tr_rmse("LightGBM")}, a
difference of about one percent. That gap is
better read as a comparison between two fitted specifications than as a stable
ranking of the algorithms themselves, and Stage~3 shows how even that ordering
shifts once recent sales information becomes available. These
results support Prediction~5 only in part. The two boosted models do lead, but third
place goes to plain bagging rather than to the random forest, so the ensemble
without feature subsampling ends up ahead of the one with it, and the ranking does not line up neatly with model complexity.
Still, flexible tree ensembles dominate the
linear specification whether or not they are regularised, and tree structure
alone already captures part of Rossmann's predictable variation, since even a
single deep CART tree improves on Store-mean.

\paragraph{Estimated store levels, fitted departures and forecast errors.}
Equation~\eqref{eq:dgp} splits daily sales into a store level, a predictable
departure and a residual. On the Stage~1 holdout their fitted counterparts can
be illustrated numerically. The store level $\hat\mu_s$ is the store's training mean. It averages
\Sexpr{.dc <- tr_read("decomposition.csv"); sprintf("%.0f", .dc$mu_mean[1])}
euros, with a standard deviation of
\Sexpr{sprintf("%.0f", .dc$mu_sd_across_stores[1])} euros across stores, so the
stores differ a lot in level. On a test day, sales depart from this level by
\Sexpr{sprintf("%.0f", .dc$departure_rms[1])} euros on average (root mean
square), which is exactly the error of Store-mean. How much of that departure
is $\delta$ depends on the information set (Section~\ref{sec:framework}). For
a forecast $\hat y_{s,t}$ that uses a given set,
$\hat\delta_{s,t} = \hat y_{s,t} - \hat\mu_s$ and
$\hat u_{s,t} = y_{s,t} - \hat y_{s,t}$, so
$y_{s,t} - \hat\mu_s = \hat\delta_{s,t} + \hat u_{s,t}$ holds for every
store-day. Table~\ref{tab:decomposition} reports both parts for three sets.
The weekly pattern alone explains
\Sexpr{sprintf("%.0f", 100 * .dc$share_delta[1])}\% of the squared departure.
Calendar, store and promotion features raise this to
\Sexpr{sprintf("%.0f", 100 * .dc$share_delta[2])}\%, and recent sales to
\Sexpr{sprintf("%.0f", 100 * .dc$share_delta[3])}\%. These are realised
reductions in squared forecast error on the holdout, not estimates of the latent
parts of Equation~\eqref{eq:dgp}. The last row also updates recent sales during
the test period, so it answers a different forecasting question from the first
two. Figure~\ref{fig:decomposition}
shows the three parts for one store.

\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\caption{Fitted prediction decomposition and test MSE reductions on the Stage~1 holdout (euros,
root mean square over all test store-days). $\hat\delta$ is the forecast minus
the store's training mean and $\hat u$ the actual sales minus the forecast. The
MSE reduction is $1 - \text{mean}(\hat u^2)/\text{mean}((y-\hat\mu)^2)$, with Store-mean and all three forecasts scored on the same store-days. Forecasts from
the Store-DOW baseline and from LightGBM as in Stages~1 and~3.}
\label{tab:decomposition}
\begin{tabular}{lrrr}
\toprule
Information set & $\hat\delta$ & $\hat u$ & MSE reduction vs Store-mean (\%) \\
\midrule
<<tab-decomposition, results='asis', cache=FALSE>>=
d <- tr_read("decomposition.csv")      # rossmann_decomposition.Rmd
for (i in seq_len(nrow(d)))
  cat(sprintf("%s & %.0f & %.0f & %.1f \\\\\n", d$Information_set[i], d$delta_rms[i],
              d$u_rms[i], 100 * d$share_delta[i]))
@
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

<<fig-decomposition, include=FALSE, cache=FALSE>>=
s1 <- tr_read("decomposition_store1.csv")      # rossmann_decomposition.Rmd
g <- ggplot(s1, aes(Date)) +
  geom_point(aes(y = Sales, colour = "Actual sales"), size = 1.6) +
  geom_line(aes(y = mu + delta, colour = "mu + delta (forecast)")) +
  geom_line(aes(y = mu, colour = "mu (store level)"), linetype = 2) +
  scale_colour_manual(values = c("Actual sales" = "black", "mu + delta (forecast)" = "#CC6677",
                                 "mu (store level)" = "#777777"), name = NULL) +
  labs(x = NULL, y = "Daily sales (EUR)") + theme_minimal(base_size = 14) +
  theme(legend.position = "top")
ggsave("rossmann_outputs/fig_decomposition_store1.pdf", g, width = 8, height = 4)
@

\begin{figure}[H]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.8\textwidth]{fig_decomposition_store1.pdf}
\caption{Store~1 on the holdout. The dashed line is $\hat\mu_s$, the line is the
forecast $\hat\mu_s + \hat\delta_{s,t}$ (LightGBM with recent sales), and the
points are actual sales. The gap between a point and the line is
$\hat u_{s,t}$.}
\label{fig:decomposition}
\end{figure}

% ------------------------------------------------------------
\subsubsection{Stage 2: Subsetting Experiments}
\label{sec:subsets_results}
% ------------------------------------------------------------

The full-sample rows in Tables~\ref{tab:stage2_n}, \ref{tab:stage2_window}
and \ref{tab:stage2_type} fit the same models to the same data, so they give
the same results. They differ slightly from Stage~1 (Table~\ref{tab:stage1}),
which was run in a separate script.

\paragraph{Dimension 1: Number of Stores.}

Table~\ref{tab:stage2_n} reports selected diagnostics for the number-of-stores
experiment. Results for 50, 200, and 500 stores are averaged over 20 random
draws. The full condition uses all 1115 stores once.

\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\caption{Stage~2 results: number-of-stores experiment. $\Delta_{\text{DOW}}$
is the gain of the store-day-of-week baseline over the store-mean baseline. The
boosted-model gains for $N \le 500$ are means over 20 random draws, with the
standard error of the mean in parentheses; the full sample is the population, so
it is run once and has no sampling error.}
\label{tab:stage2_n}
\resizebox{\textwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{rrrrrr}
\toprule
$N$ & Na\"{i}ve RMSE & Store-DOW RMSE & Store-DOW $\Delta$ (\%) &
XGBoost $\Delta_{\text{rel}}$ (\%) & LightGBM $\Delta_{\text{rel}}$ (\%) \\
\midrule
<<tab-stage2-n, results='asis', cache=FALSE>>=
d <- tr_read("stage2_nstores.csv")     # rossmann_stages2_4.Rmd
if (is.null(d)) {
  tt_missing(6, "stage2_nstores.csv", "rossmann_stages2_4.Rmd")
} else {
  # Standard errors of the five-draw means come from the Stage 6 repetition
  # file (rossmann_stage6.Rmd), which reports the same draws.
  # rossmann_stages2_4.Rmd writes the standard error of each five-draw mean
  # next to the mean (NA for the single full-panel run).
  if (!all(c("XGB_delta_se", "LGBM_delta_se") %in% names(d)))
    stop("stage2_nstores.csv has no standard-error columns: re-knit rossmann_stages2_4.Rmd")
  d$XGB_delta_sd  <- d$XGB_delta_se
  d$LGBM_delta_sd <- d$LGBM_delta_se
  has_sd <- TRUE
  for (i in seq_len(nrow(d))) {
    x <- d[i, ]
    xg <- tt_num(x$XGB_delta);  lg <- tt_num(x$LGBM_delta)
    # The full-panel row is a single run, so it carries no replication SD.
    if (has_sd && !is.na(x$XGB_delta_sd))
      xg <- sprintf("%s (%s)", xg, tt_num(x$XGB_delta_sd))
    if (has_sd && !is.na(x$LGBM_delta_sd))
      lg <- sprintf("%s (%s)", lg, tt_num(x$LGBM_delta_sd))
    cat(sprintf("%d & %.0f & %.0f & %s & %s & %s \\\\\n",
                as.integer(x$N), x$Naive_RMSE, x$StoreDOW_RMSE,
                tt_num(x$StoreDOW_delta), xg, lg))
  }
}
@
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.68\textwidth]{fig_stage2_nstores_gain.png}
\caption{Stage~2 number-of-stores experiment: improvement over the store-mean
baseline for the Store-DOW na\"{i}ve benchmark and the two boosted models.}
\label{fig:stage2_nstores_gain}
\end{figure}

Prediction~1 is rejected. XGBoost's gain falls from
\Sexpr{.n <- tr_read("stage2_nstores.csv"); tt_num(.n$XGB_delta[1])}\% at 50 stores
to \Sexpr{tt_num(.n$XGB_delta[nrow(.n)])}\% in the full panel, while LightGBM falls
from \Sexpr{tt_num(.n$LGBM_delta[1])}\% to \Sexpr{tt_num(.n$LGBM_delta[nrow(.n)])}\%.
Store-DOW remains about 14\% better than Store-mean across the four
conditions. The observed relationship is the reverse of the predicted
monotonic increase, and the decline is significant. Within a draw the store
samples are nested, so the tests pair by draw. Regressing the gain on the
logarithm of the number of stores with draw fixed effects gives a slope of
\Sexpr{.ts <- tr_read("stage2_nstores_tests.csv"); .tx <- .ts[.ts$Model == "XGBoost", ]; .tl <- .ts[.ts$Model == "LightGBM", ]; tt_neg(.tx$slope_per_log_N, 2)} (standard error
\Sexpr{tt_num(.tx$slope_se, 2)}, \Sexpr{.pv <- function(p) if (p < 0.001) "p < 0.001" else paste0("p = ", formatC(p, format = "f", digits = 3)); .pv(.tx$p_two_sided)}) for
XGBoost and \Sexpr{tt_neg(.tl$slope_per_log_N, 2)} (standard error
\Sexpr{tt_num(.tl$slope_se, 2)}, \Sexpr{.pv(.tl$p_two_sided)}) for LightGBM, and a
paired test of 500 against 50 stores gives the same result
(\Sexpr{.pv(.tx$paired_p)} and \Sexpr{.pv(.tl$paired_p)}). The predicted increase
is therefore contradicted in this mixed-store subsampling design. Because the
evaluated stores change together with the training pool, this does not show
that adding training stores itself makes forecasts worse. The experiment shows the pattern but cannot say how much of it comes from the
changing store mix and how much from baseline strength.
Section~\ref{sec:global_local} repeats it with the store type held fixed,
although stores of the same type still differ in other ways.

\paragraph{Dimension 2: Training Window Length.}

Table~\ref{tab:stage2_window} reports $\Delta_{\text{rel}}$ as the training
window is progressively extended.

