\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.68\textwidth]{fig_stage2_window_gain.png}
\caption{Stage~2 training-window experiment: improvement over the store-mean
baseline as the available training history increases.}
\label{fig:stage2_window_gain}
\end{figure}

Prediction~2 is supported only at the lower end of the training range. Both
models do worst with six months of training data, and from 12 months onward
the gains are stable, with XGBoost between
\Sexpr{.w <- tr_read("stage2_window.csv"); tt_num(min(.w$XGB_delta[-1]))}\% and
\Sexpr{tt_num(max(.w$XGB_delta[-1]))}\% and LightGBM between
\Sexpr{tt_num(min(.w$LGBM_delta[-1]))}\% and \Sexpr{tt_num(max(.w$LGBM_delta[-1]))}\%.
The largest improvement comes between six and 12 months. Adding further
history does not give the predicted steady increase.

The six-month window shows why one full year matters. LightGBM still improves
on Store-mean by \Sexpr{tt_num(.w$LGBM_delta[1])}\%, but XGBoost reaches
\Sexpr{tt_signed(.w$XGB_delta[1])}\%, against a gap of only
\Sexpr{tt_num(abs(.w$XGB_delta[nrow(.w)] - .w$LGBM_delta[nrow(.w)]))} points at
full history. Six months before the test period runs from late December to
mid-June, so the training data contain weeks 1 to
\Sexpr{.g <- tr_read("stage2_window_diag.csv"); .g$last_train_week} and the
December weeks, but not a single July day. Week enters the model as a number,
and a tree can only split it at a value seen in training. Every test week, from
\Sexpr{.g$test_week_min} to \Sexpr{.g$test_week_max}, therefore goes to the
same branch as week \Sexpr{.g$last_train_week} in every tree, so the model
treats the whole test period as week \Sexpr{.g$last_train_week}. That is the
last training week. It has only \Sexpr{tt_words(.g$last_week_days)} days, and
\Sexpr{sprintf("%.0f", 100 * .g$last_week_promo_share)}\% of its rows are
promotion days.

The six-month XGBoost forecasts are too high almost everywhere: the mean forecast is
\Sexpr{sprintf("%.0f", .g$xgb_pred_mean)} against actual sales of
\Sexpr{sprintf("%.0f", .g$actual_mean)}, and
\Sexpr{sprintf("%.0f", 100 * .g$share_stores_over)}\% of the stores are
overpredicted. Sales did not fall, because Store-mean is too high by only
\Sexpr{sprintf("%.0f", .g$storemean_bias)}, and on the training data the
model's average error is close to zero in every month. Splitting each forecast
into the part each input adds shows that Week accounts for
\Sexpr{sprintf("%.0f", .g$shap_week)} of the
\Sexpr{sprintf("%.0f", .g$shap_total)} euros by which the test forecasts exceed those
for May and June. The direct test confirms it. When only Week is moved to an
earlier training week (20, 23 or \Sexpr{.g$last_train_week - 1}), the gain
rises to between \Sexpr{tt_num(.g$cf_week_min_gain)}\% and
\Sexpr{tt_num(.g$cf_week_max_gain)}\%, close to LightGBM. The overprediction is
also largest on days without a promotion
(\Sexpr{sprintf("%.0f", .g$bias_promo0)} against
\Sexpr{sprintf("%.0f", .g$bias_promo1)} on promotion days), which fits a model
that forecasts the test period from a week made of promotion days.

A model trained on less than one annual cycle has not seen the calendar
positions it is asked to forecast, and a tree model then forecasts them as the
nearest position it has seen.

\paragraph{Dimension 3: Store-Type Composition.}

Table~\ref{tab:stage2_type} reports results for four store-type subsets.

\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\caption{Stage~2 results: store-type composition and baseline strength.
Type~A comprises 602 stores and
Type~B 17. The C$+$D mix comprises 496 stores (148 Type~C and 348 Type~D);
Full includes all 1115 stores across types A, B, C, and D.}
\label{tab:stage2_type}
\resizebox{\textwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{lrrrrr}
\toprule
Subset & Na\"{i}ve RMSE & Store-DOW RMSE & Store-DOW $\Delta$ (\%) &
XGBoost $\Delta_{\text{rel}}$ (\%) & LightGBM $\Delta_{\text{rel}}$ (\%) \\
\midrule
<<tab-stage2-type, results='asis', cache=FALSE>>=
d <- tr_read("stage2_storetype.csv")   # rossmann_stages2_4.Rmd
if (is.null(d)) {
  tt_missing(6, "stage2_storetype.csv", "rossmann_stages2_4.Rmd")
} else {
  for (i in seq_len(nrow(d))) {
    x <- d[i, ]
    lab <- gsub("+", "$+$", x$Condition, fixed = TRUE)
    cat(sprintf("%s & %.0f & %.0f & %s & %s & %s \\\\\n", lab,
                x$Naive_RMSE, x$StoreDOW_RMSE, tt_num(x$StoreDOW_delta),
                tt_num(x$XGB_delta), tt_num(x$LGBM_delta)))
  }
}
@
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

Prediction~3 is not supported. Type~B has the largest baseline errors and the
largest gains for both boosted models. Part of its larger \emph{absolute}
RMSE simply comes from Type~B's higher sales level, so the percentage measures
are the cleaner evidence. Store-DOW improves on Store-mean by 30.9\% in this
group, and day-of-week means explain about 39\% of its within-store variance.
Both point to weekly structure as a large part of its baseline error. This
holds for the 17 Type~B stores in the data; with so few stores it should not
be generalised far. The balanced C$+$D mix, by contrast, gains about as much
as Type~A (XGBoost \Sexpr{.t <- tr_read("stage2_storetype.csv"); tt_num(.t$XGB_delta[.t$Condition == "C+D"])}\%
against \Sexpr{tt_num(.t$XGB_delta[.t$Condition == "A only"])}\%, LightGBM
\Sexpr{tt_num(.t$LGBM_delta[.t$Condition == "C+D"])}\% against
\Sexpr{tt_num(.t$LGBM_delta[.t$Condition == "A only"])}\%), so mixing two mid-size
types leaves the advantage unchanged. Only the distinctive Type~B stands apart.

The descriptive evidence in Table~\ref{tab:storetypes} explains why the
prediction fails. Type~B stores are open on almost every Sunday and have
average sales well above the other types, so the group labelled most
homogeneous by store type is the group with the largest and most
distinctive weekly variation. In the terms of the framework, an administrative
store label is not a proxy for low $\text{Var}(\delta_{s,t})$: Type~B has the
\emph{highest} predictable within-store variation of any group. Day-of-week means alone
explain about 39\% of its within-store variance on average, but the Store-DOW baseline already
removes that weekly part, so the boosted models' large gain \emph{over Store-DOW}
there comes from the promotion and calendar structure beyond the weekly cycle.
This is consistent with Section~\ref{sec:properties}, where the per-store gain
rises with the broader predictable share and falls with the purely weekly share.
The lack of support for Prediction~3 is therefore a result about the store-type
heuristic used to derive it, not an isolated test of heterogeneity, and it fits
Equation~\eqref{eq:gap}.

% ------------------------------------------------------------
\subsubsection{Stage 3: Lag Feature Enrichment}
% ------------------------------------------------------------

Table~\ref{tab:stage3} compares model performance before and after adding the
six lag and rolling-mean features defined in
Section~\ref{sec:phase2}.

\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\caption{Stage~3 results: $\Delta_{\text{rel}}$ with and without lag features,
full Rossmann dataset. $\Delta_{\text{DOW}}$ reports improvement over the
store-day-of-week baseline.}
\label{tab:stage3}
\begin{tabular}{lrrrrr}
\toprule
 & \multicolumn{2}{c}{Stage~1 (no lags)} & \multicolumn{3}{c}{Stage~3 (with lags)} \\
\cmidrule(lr){2-3}\cmidrule(lr){4-6}
Model     & RMSE    & $\Delta_{\text{rel}}$ (\%) & RMSE  & $\Delta_{\text{rel}}$ (\%) & $\Delta_{\text{DOW}}$ (\%) \\
\midrule
<<tab-stage3, results='asis', cache=FALSE>>=
# Two halves: the no-lag columns are the Stage 1 run, the with-lag columns are
# the Stage 3 run in rossmann_stages2_4.Rmd.
nolag <- tr_read("stage1_pooled_models.csv")
lag   <- tr_read("stage3_lags.csv")
if (is.null(nolag) || is.null(lag)) {
  tt_missing(6, "stage1_pooled_models.csv / stage3_lags.csv",
             "rossmann_stage1_models.Rmd, rossmann_stages2_4.Rmd")
} else {
