\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.72\textwidth]{fig_stage3_rmse.png}
\caption{Stage~3 RMSE comparison before and after lag feature enrichment.
Adding lag and rolling-mean features substantially reduces the error of both
boosted models.}
\label{fig:stage3_rmse}
\end{figure}

Prediction~4 is supported. Adding lags raises XGBoost's improvement from
\Sexpr{tr_dow("XGBoost")}\% to \Sexpr{tr_lagd("XGBoost")}\% and LightGBM's from
\Sexpr{tr_dow("LightGBM")}\% to \Sexpr{tr_lagd("LightGBM")}\%. The two boosted models
are already close without lags and stay within a few percent of each other
after enrichment (LightGBM \Sexpr{tr_lag("LightGBM")}, XGBoost
\Sexpr{tr_lag("XGBoost")}), with both still far ahead of
Store-DOW. These figures use rolling one-day-ahead updating, so part of the
lag-feature gain comes from updating the lag inputs with actual sales each day, not from the features
alone. The fixed-origin counterpart in Stage~6 (LightGBM \Sexpr{.e1 <- tr_read("stage6_e1.csv"); .r1 <- setNames(.e1[["Stage3b recursive"]], .e1$Model); .f1 <- setNames(.e1[["Stage1 fixed-origin"]], .e1$Model); sprintf("%.0f", .r1["LightGBM"])}, XGBoost \Sexpr{sprintf("%.0f", .r1["XGBoost"])})
isolates the smaller, but still clear, contribution of the lags at a frozen
forecast origin.

In the fitted models, lag and rolling-mean variables account for about 73\% of
LightGBM's gain-based feature importance and about 74\% of XGBoost's. This
share shows where each model puts its splits once the lags are available; it is
not separate evidence. Gain-based importance also depends on the exact measure
used and is affected by correlated features. With that caveat, it points the
same way as the RMSE reduction and supports Prediction~4.

% ------------------------------------------------------------
\subsubsection{Stage 4: Robustness Across Rolling Test Windows}
% ------------------------------------------------------------

The final analysis examines whether the Stage~1 ranking is specific to the
June--July 2015 holdout period. Table~\ref{tab:rolling_robustness} reports the
results from four 42-day test windows. Three fall in spring and summer 2015. The
fourth covers 30 November 2014 to 10 January 2015, so that the December sales
peak, the strongest seasonal pattern in the data, is also tested. All models are
re-estimated in each window using only observations dated before the
corresponding test period, so this window is trained on data up to late
November 2014, having already seen the previous year's peak.

\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\caption{Rolling 42-day test-window robustness results (RMSE). Window 4 covers
the December peak. Window 1 is the Stage~1 holdout, but the models are refitted
separately here, so its values can differ slightly from Table~\ref{tab:stage1}.}
\label{tab:rolling_robustness}
\resizebox{\textwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{llrrrr}
\toprule
Window & Test period & Store mean & Store-DOW & XGBoost & LightGBM \\
\midrule
<<tab-rolling, results='asis', cache=FALSE>>=
