\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.72\textwidth]{fig_rolling_window_robustness}
\caption{RMSE across four 42-day test windows. Each model is estimated using only
observations available before the relevant test period.}
\label{fig:rolling_robustness}
\end{figure}

Both boosted models lie far below the two baselines in every window, reducing
RMSE over Store-mean by roughly
\Sexpr{.r <- tr_read("stage4_rolling.csv"); .d <- tail(tr_read("rolling_december.csv"), 1); .g <- c(1 - c(.r$XGBoost, .r$LightGBM) / rep(.r$Store_mean, 2), 1 - c(.d$XGBoost, .d$LightGBM) / .d$Store_mean) * 100; sprintf("%.0f", floor(min(.g)))}
to \Sexpr{sprintf("%.0f", ceiling(max(.g)))}\%. The advantage is largest in
the December window. There the simple baselines are hit hardest by the seasonal
peak, and Store-DOW rises to 2672. The boosted models instead capture the peak
through the month indicator and the prior year they have already seen, and they
improve on Store-DOW by 44 to 45\%. Absolute errors rise for every model in this high-volume
period, but the relative advantage gets larger. XGBoost
and LightGBM stay within \Sexpr{.r <- tr_read("stage4_rolling.csv"); .d <- tail(tr_read("rolling_december.csv"), 1); .x <- c(.r$XGBoost, .d$XGBoost); .l <- c(.r$LightGBM, .d$LightGBM); sprintf("%.0f", ceiling(max(abs(.x - .l) / pmin(.x, .l)) * 100))}\% of each other in RMSE in every window, so the
robust finding is the gap between the boosted models and the baselines, not the
order within the pair.

So the result holds over four periods, including the strongest seasonal
window. Four windows do not cover every possible forecast origin, but they show
that the main ranking does not hang on a single holdout and that the advantage
is not an artefact of the spring and summer season.

% ------------------------------------------------------------
\subsubsection{Stage 5: From a History-Only to a Full Information Set}
% ------------------------------------------------------------
\label{sec:incremental_results}

Table~\ref{tab:stage5} reports the incremental information experiment. Each
row pair shows XGBoost and LightGBM re-estimated on one of the four nested
feature sets described in Section~\ref{sec:incremental_design}.

\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\caption{Stage~5 results: fixed-origin 42-day forecasts under nested
information sets. No test-period sales enter any predictor.
$\Delta_{\text{DOW}}$ is the percentage RMSE improvement over Store-DOW
(RMSE 1647 on this split); negative values mean the model is worse than the
benchmark.}
\label{tab:stage5}
\resizebox{0.8\textwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{llrrr}
\toprule
Information set & Model & RMSE & MAPE (\%) & $\Delta_{\text{DOW}}$ (\%) \\
\midrule
<<tab-stage5, results='asis', cache=FALSE>>=
d <- tr_read("stage5_information_sets.csv")    # rossmann_stage5.Rmd
if (is.null(d)) {
  tt_missing(5, "stage5_information_sets.csv", "rossmann_stage5.Rmd")
} else {
  setcol <- names(d)[1]                   # "Information set"
  labs <- d[[setcol]]
  prev <- ""
  for (i in seq_len(nrow(d))) {
    x <- d[i, ]
    # "1 History only" -> "1. History only"; "+" needs math mode in LaTeX.
    lab <- sub("^([0-9]+)\\s+", "\\1. ", labs[i])
    lab <- gsub("+", "$+$", lab, fixed = TRUE)
    show <- if (identical(lab, prev)) "" else lab
    prev <- lab
    cat(sprintf("%s & %s & %.0f & %s & %s \\\\\n",
                show, x$Model, x$RMSE, tt_num(x$MAPE), tt_signed(x$Delta_DOW)))
  }
