\end{table}

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.72\textwidth]{fig_stage5_information_path.png}
\caption{Stage~5 information path: test RMSE of XGBoost and LightGBM across the
four nested information sets, with the Store-DOW reference level reconstructed
from the reported percentage gains (rounding applies).}
\label{fig:stage5}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:stage5} traces these RMSE values across the four nested sets.
The history-only condition is the most revealing. With nothing but store
identity and origin-safe summaries of its own sales, XGBoost and LightGBM are
\Sexpr{tr_s5d("1", "XGBoost")}\% and \Sexpr{tr_s5d("1", "LightGBM")}\% \emph{worse} than Store-DOW (RMSE
\Sexpr{tr_s5("1", "XGBoost")} and \Sexpr{tr_s5("1", "LightGBM")} against 1647)
and far behind the store-level Auto-ARIMA fits
(\Sexpr{tr_rmse("ARIMA")}). In a univariate information
environment, a well-specified statistical model fitted to each series is
clearly the better choice. This echoes, inside a single dataset, the pattern
observed in the early M competitions, and it suggests that the weak
machine-learning results of that period reflected more than algorithm quality,
since even current implementations of XGBoost and LightGBM can do little with
this information set.

Adding calendar variables changes the picture sharply. Both models drop well
below Auto-ARIMA (XGBoost \Sexpr{tr_s5("2", "XGBoost")}, LightGBM
\Sexpr{tr_s5("2", "LightGBM")}), and LightGBM gains more from
the calendar information than XGBoost. Store attributes do not help and
set XGBoost back, raising its RMSE from \Sexpr{tr_s5("2", "XGBoost")} to
\Sexpr{tr_s5("3", "XGBoost")}, while LightGBM improves only marginally
(\Sexpr{tr_s5("2", "LightGBM")} to \Sexpr{tr_s5("3", "LightGBM")}). This is what one would expect once the store
identifier has already absorbed the stable differences between stores. Type,
assortment, and competitor variables then have little within-store predictive
content left to add. Why the two models react differently, for example through
capacity or feature selection, is not something this experiment can isolate.
Promotion and holiday indicators then improve both fits, to
\Sexpr{tr_s5("4", "XGBoost")} and \Sexpr{tr_s5("4", "LightGBM")}. Calendar
variables give the largest single drop for both models, although this depends
partly on the order in which the feature groups are added.

This progression supports Prediction~6. With only its own sales history each
boosted model does no better than the simple and statistical baselines, and the
ensemble advantage grows as calendar, store, and promotion information is added.

\paragraph{Testing the reversal formally.} The claim above, trees behind
Auto-ARIMA with history only, trees ahead with the full feature set, is so
far only a comparison of RMSE point estimates. The same Diebold--Mariano
procedure used in Section~\ref{sec:additional} (Equation~\eqref{eq:dm}) is
therefore applied to both regimes. Here $e^{(1)}_t$ is the boosted model's daily
loss and $e^{(2)}_t$ is Auto-ARIMA's, so a negative $\mathrm{DM}$ statistic
means the boosted model has the lower loss and a positive one means Auto-ARIMA
does:
\begin{equation}
    H_0: \mathbb{E}\!\left[e^{(1)}_t - e^{(2)}_t\right] = 0
    , \qquad
    H_1: \mathbb{E}\!\left[e^{(1)}_t - e^{(2)}_t\right] \neq 0.
    \label{eq:dm_treevsstat}
\end{equation}
The two feature regimes do not use exactly the same stores and dates, because
each is trimmed to its own warm-up needs: the origin-safe lags of Stage~5 need
84 trading observations of earlier history per store, and Stage~1 needs none.
The two sets of tree predictions and the Auto-ARIMA forecasts are therefore
inner-joined on (store, date) before testing, so every test in
Table~\ref{tab:stage7} compares the three models on the same rows within its
regime. The tests are run in \path{rossmann_stage7_tree_vs_statistical.Rmd}, a
separate file, because the Stage~5 pipeline does not keep row-level
predictions and never fits Auto-ARIMA, so no earlier file has the aligned
forecasts that a paired test needs.

\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\caption{Diebold--Mariano tests (Equation~\eqref{eq:dm_treevsstat}) for the
tree-versus-statistical reversal, full-feature (Stage~1) and history-only
(Stage~5, condition 1) regimes. Negative $\mathrm{DM}$ favours the boosted
model. Positive favours Auto-ARIMA. $p$-values Holm-adjusted across all four
tests.}
\label{tab:stage7}
\begin{tabular}{lrrl}
\toprule
Comparison & DM & Holm $p$ & Favours \\
\midrule
<<tab-stage7, results='asis', cache=FALSE>>=
d <- tr_read("stage7_dm_tests.csv")    # rossmann_stage7_tree_vs_statistical.Rmd
if (is.null(d)) {
  tt_missing(4, "stage7_dm_tests.csv", "rossmann_stage7_tree_vs_statistical.Rmd")
} else {
  for (i in seq_len(nrow(d))) {
    x <- d[i, ]
    # A negative DM statistic favours the first-named model, a positive one
    # favours Auto-ARIMA.
    fav <- if (x$DM < 0) sub("^.*: *([A-Za-z]+) +vs.*$", "\\1", x$Comparison) else "Auto-ARIMA"
    p   <- if (x$p_holm < 0.001) "$<0.001$" else sprintf("$%.3f$", x$p_holm)
    cat(sprintf("%s & $%.2f$ & %s & %s \\\\\n",
                gsub("_", "\\\\_", x$Comparison), x$DM, p, fav))
  }
}
@
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

All four tests have Holm-adjusted $p<0.01$ on the aligned rows. The same file
also recomputes the RMSEs: about 1003 and 1013 for full-feature XGBoost and
LightGBM, and 1840 and 1852 for their history-only fits, against 1508 for
ARIMA. The history-only XGBoost value differs from Stage~5's 1843 because these
are separate fits. The reversal holds, but the tests use \texttt{h=1}, so their
variance estimate ignores dependence between neighbouring days. They should be
read as nominal evidence for this setting, not as a final significance result
for the model families.

The best fixed-origin RMSE here (\Sexpr{tr_s5("4", "LightGBM")}) is higher than
the Stage~1 no-lag result (\Sexpr{tr_rmse("LightGBM")}): the origin-safe
history features, whose shortest lag is 42 trading observations, carry
less signal than the calendar and promotion variables already present in
Stage~1, and the warm-up rows dropped to construct them shrink the training
sample. The rolling Stage~3 result (\Sexpr{tr_lag("LightGBM")}) remains far better than any
fixed-origin specification, so a large part of the Stage~3 advantage reflects
daily updating rather than information available at the origin. The sequential
attribution in Table~\ref{tab:stage5} also depends on the order in which
feature groups are added. The order used here follows the historical
development from univariate to covariate-rich environments, and
single-condition results should be read with that in mind.

\subsubsection{Covariate-Matched ARIMAX Robustness Check}
\label{sec:arimax_check}

% The ARIMAX experiment is not run during knitting. Knit rossmann_arimax.Rmd
% once beforehand; it writes thesis_tables/arimax_comparison.csv and
% arimax_uncertainty.csv, and the two tables and the \Sexpr numbers in this
% section only read them.
Up to this point the statistical models see only each store's past sales,
while the trees also see the calendar and the promotion plan. Part of the tree
advantage could therefore come from this extra information rather than from
the trees themselves. This check removes that difference. Regression with
ARIMA errors (ARIMAX), XGBoost and LightGBM all receive the same covariates:
weekday indicators, promotion and its interaction with the weekday, school and
state holidays, whether the store is open, an annual sine and cosine pair and
a linear time trend. ARIMAX is fitted separately for every store, so each
store gets its own covariate effects. The trees are fitted once on all stores
together and also receive store indicators. Opening days and the promotion
plan are treated as known in advance, which is how the Kaggle test file
provides them.

The store sample is fixed before any model is fitted. Only stores with a
complete daily record up to the first origin can be used, and 50 of them are
drawn with seed 230923, stratified by type as 27 A, 1 B, 7 C and 15 D.
Forecasts start on 8 May and on 19 June 2015 and run for 42 days. Every model
trains on the same daily rows, closed-day zeros included, and every model is
scored on the same open days with positive sales. No test sales are used.
Auto-ARIMA without covariates is fitted on the same stores, so the effect of
the covariates alone can be read inside this experiment. Because the
experiment uses only 50 stores, its RMSE values should not be compared one to
one with the full-panel results earlier in this section.

ARIMA orders are chosen by AICc with a stepwise search, with $p,q\leq3$,
$P,Q\leq1$ and a combined order of at most 5. Covariate columns that are
constant or duplicated in the training window are dropped, using training
data only. For the trees, the number of rounds is chosen on the last 42
training days, and the model is then refitted on the whole training window
with that number. Tree size is fixed at depth 6 for XGBoost and 31 leaves for
LightGBM. The information is matched in this way, but not everything else:
the model families, their loss functions and the amount of tuning still
differ, and ARIMAX is still local while the trees are pooled.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Covariate-matched comparison: 50 stores, two fixed origins. All
models at an origin are scored on the same store--day rows.}
\label{tab:arimax}
\small
\begin{tabular}{llrrr}
\toprule
Origin & Model & RMSE (EUR) & MAE (EUR) & Test rows \\
\midrule
<<arimax-table, results='asis', cache=FALSE>>=
arx <- tr_read_arx("arimax_comparison.csv")   # rossmann_arimax.Rmd
arx <- arx[order(arx$Origin,arx$RMSE),]
for(i in seq_len(nrow(arx))){x<-arx[i,];cat(sprintf("%s & %s & %.0f & %.0f & %d \\\\\n",as.character(x$Origin),x$Model,x$RMSE,x$MAE,x$N))}
@
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Paired loss differences with 95\% circular block-bootstrap intervals.
Each difference is the comparator's MSE minus the ARIMAX MSE, in $10^3$
EUR$^2$, so positive values favour ARIMAX. The bootstrap resamples whole days
in seven-day blocks, 2000 times, which keeps the dependence between nearby
days and between stores on the same day.}
\label{tab:arimax_ci}
\small
\begin{tabular}{llrrr}
\toprule
Origin & Comparator & Difference & Lower & Upper \\
\midrule
<<arimax-ci, results='asis', cache=FALSE>>=
ci <- tr_read_arx("arimax_uncertainty.csv")   # rossmann_arimax.Rmd
# $-$ gives a typeset minus sign instead of a hyphen.
.mn <- function(v, d = 1) sub("^-", "$-$", formatC(v, format = "f", digits = d))
for(i in seq_len(nrow(ci))){x<-ci[i,];cat(sprintf("%s & %s & %s & %s & %s \\\\\n",as.character(x$Origin),sub(" minus ARIMAX","",x$Comparison),.mn(x$MSE_difference/1000),.mn(x$Lower/1000),.mn(x$Upper/1000)))}
@
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Adding the covariates helps ARIMAX a lot: it beats Auto-ARIMA by a wide
margin at both origins. At the May origin its RMSE is
\Sexpr{tr_arx("2015-05-08","ARIMAX")}, against
\Sexpr{tr_arx("2015-05-08","LightGBM")} for LightGBM and
\Sexpr{tr_arx("2015-05-08","XGBoost")} for XGBoost. The paired interval for
LightGBM against ARIMAX \Sexpr{tr_arx_ci("2015-05-08","LightGBM")}, and the
one for XGBoost against ARIMAX \Sexpr{tr_arx_ci("2015-05-08","XGBoost")}.
At the June origin the picture turns around. ARIMAX reaches
\Sexpr{tr_arx("2015-06-19","ARIMAX")}, LightGBM
\Sexpr{tr_arx("2015-06-19","LightGBM")} and XGBoost
\Sexpr{tr_arx("2015-06-19","XGBoost")}; the interval for LightGBM
\Sexpr{tr_arx_ci("2015-06-19","LightGBM")} and the one for XGBoost
\Sexpr{tr_arx_ci("2015-06-19","XGBoost")}. The interval for Auto-ARIMA against
ARIMAX
\Sexpr{a1 <- tr_arx_ci("2015-05-08","Auto-ARIMA"); a2 <- tr_arx_ci("2015-06-19","Auto-ARIMA"); if (identical(a1, a2)) paste(a1, "at both origins") else paste0(a1, " at the May origin and ", a2, " at the June origin")}.
So once the statistical model gets the same information, a large part of the
gap disappears, and which model wins depends on the forecast period.
Statistical models do not simply lose once they are given useful covariates.

These intervals hold for this store sample and these two origins only. A
42-day holdout contains just six seven-day blocks, so the sampling
uncertainty is large. Type B has a single store in the sample, so nothing can
be said about that type. The question here is whether ARIMAX closes the gap
to the trees, not which family has to win. Answering it more generally would
need the same design on more origins and on other datasets.

\paragraph{Shorter training histories.}
One difference between the two families is left. The trees learn from all
50 stores at once, while each ARIMAX model sees only its own store. Learning
across stores should help most when each store has little history of its own.
To test this, the same design is run again with only 3, 6 or 12 months of
training data before the same origins
(\texttt{rossmann\_arimax\_history.Rmd}). Everything else stays the same, with
one exception: the annual sine and cosine terms are dropped from all models
when the window is shorter than a year, because with less than one yearly cycle
they are poorly determined and easily confused with the trend. The covariate set
is therefore not identical across window lengths. Table~\ref{tab:arimax_history} shows the results.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{ARIMAX and pooled trees under shorter training histories: RMSE (EUR)
on the same 50 stores and test rows. The last three columns give LightGBM MSE
minus ARIMAX MSE in $10^3$ EUR$^2$ and the bounds of its 95\% circular
block-bootstrap interval; negative values favour LightGBM. Full-history rows repeat
Table~\ref{tab:arimax}.}
\label{tab:arimax_history}
\footnotesize
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{3pt}
\begin{tabular}{llrrrrrrr}
\toprule
 & & \multicolumn{4}{c}{RMSE (EUR)} & \multicolumn{3}{c}{LightGBM $-$ ARIMAX} \\
\cmidrule(lr){3-6}\cmidrule(lr){7-9}
Origin & History & ARIMAX & LightGBM & XGBoost & Store-DOW & Diff. & Lower & Upper \\
\midrule
<<arimax-history-table, results='asis', cache=FALSE>>=
h  <- tr_read_arx("arimax_history.csv")      # rossmann_arimax_history.Rmd
hc <- tr_read_arx("arimax_history_ci.csv")
wins <- c("3 months", "6 months", "12 months", "Full history")
for (o in sort(unique(as.character(h$Origin)))) for (w in wins) {
  g <- h[as.character(h$Origin) == o & h$Window == w, ]
  r <- hc[as.character(hc$Origin) == o & hc$Window == w, ]
  v <- function(m) { x <- g$RMSE[g$Model == m]; if (length(x)) sprintf("%.0f", x) else "--" }
  mn <- function(x) sub("^-", "$-$", sprintf("%.0f", x / 1000))   # typeset minus
  cat(sprintf("%s & %s & %s & %s & %s & %s & %s & %s & %s \\\\\n",
              if (w == wins[1]) o else "", w, v("ARIMAX"), v("LightGBM"),
              v("XGBoost"), v("Store-DOW"), mn(r$MSE_difference[1]),
              mn(r$Lower[1]), mn(r$Upper[1])))
  if (w == wins[4] && o != max(as.character(h$Origin))) cat("\\midrule\n")
}
@
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

A shorter history does not make pooling win more often. At the May origin,
\Sexpr{tr_hist_n("lgbm","2015-05-08")} of the four intervals favour LightGBM
and \Sexpr{tr_hist_n("arimax","2015-05-08")} favour ARIMAX. At the June
origin, \Sexpr{tr_hist_n("lgbm","2015-06-19")} favour LightGBM and
\Sexpr{tr_hist_n("arimax","2015-06-19")} favour ARIMAX. Over all
\Sexpr{tr_hist_n("all")} combinations of origin and window, LightGBM is
significantly better in \Sexpr{tr_hist_n("lgbm")} and ARIMAX in
\Sexpr{tr_hist_n("arimax")}. On average the RMSE is
\Sexpr{tr_hist_rmse("mean","LightGBM")} for LightGBM and
\Sexpr{tr_hist_rmse("mean","ARIMAX")} for ARIMAX. ARIMAX also moves around
more: its RMSE lies between \Sexpr{tr_hist_rmse("min","ARIMAX")} and
\Sexpr{tr_hist_rmse("max","ARIMAX")}, against
\Sexpr{tr_hist_rmse("min","LightGBM")} and
\Sexpr{tr_hist_rmse("max","LightGBM")} for LightGBM. All windows use the same
two test periods, so this is really two independent comparisons and not
eight. What decides the order is the forecast period, not the length of the
training history.

\paragraph{Why the tree models remain the main models.}
This result supports the main argument of the thesis more than it ranks the
two families. With the same covariates, the same statistical model gets much
better: ARIMAX lowers the Auto-ARIMA RMSE by
\Sexpr{tr_arx_gain("2015-05-08")}\% at the May origin and
\Sexpr{tr_arx_gain("2015-06-19")}\% at the June origin. Stage~5 shows the same
thing for the trees when calendar, promotion and store information is added.
The conclusion therefore does not depend on the method: most of the gap
between the univariate benchmarks and the trees comes from the information
the models get, not from the model class.

ARIMAX is still not used in the other experiments, for two reasons. First, a
per-store ARIMAX forecast does not change when other stores are added or
removed, because each model sees only its own store. The scale, heterogeneity
and global-versus-local experiments of Stage~2 and
Section~\ref{sec:global_local} are about exactly that, so ARIMAX could only serve
there as a fixed reference. Second, the ARIMAX pipeline used here requires a
complete daily record. Only \Sexpr{tr_arx_eligible()} of the 1115 stores have
one up to the first origin, while the trees can use every store as it is.
Even with the same information, ARIMAX is not significantly better than
LightGBM in any combination of origin and window, and its errors change more
from one forecast period to the next.

\subsubsection{Stage 6: Additional Analyses}
\label{sec:additional}

Six further analyses check how solid the main results are. They are run in a
separate file (\texttt{rossmann\_stage6.Rmd}) with the same pipeline as the
other stages. The recursive-lag analysis below compares against the Stage~1
no-lag values (XGBoost \Sexpr{tr_rmse("XGBoost")}, LightGBM
\Sexpr{tr_rmse("LightGBM")}). The hyperparameter grid is a separate run, so
its settings closest to Stage~1 do not reproduce those values exactly.

\paragraph{Fixed-Origin Recursive Lag Forecasts.}

Stage~3 changes two things at once: the lag features add information, and the
rolling scheme adds daily updating. To separate them, the lag models are run
again with recursive forecasts. During the 42-day holdout the lag and rolling
features are computed from the model's own predictions, so nothing after the
forecast origin is used.

Estimated this way, LightGBM reaches an RMSE of \Sexpr{.e1 <- tr_read("stage6_e1.csv"); .r1 <- setNames(.e1[["Stage3b recursive"]], .e1$Model); .f1 <- setNames(.e1[["Stage1 fixed-origin"]], .e1$Model); sprintf("%.0f", .r1["LightGBM"])} and XGBoost \Sexpr{sprintf("%.0f", .r1["XGBoost"])},
compared with \Sexpr{tr_rmse("LightGBM")} and \Sexpr{tr_rmse("XGBoost")}
without lags. Lag information available at the
origin therefore accounts for an improvement of about \Sexpr{sprintf("%.0f", 100 * (1 - .r1["LightGBM"] / .f1["LightGBM"]))}\% for LightGBM but only
about \Sexpr{sprintf("%.0f", 100 * (1 - .r1["XGBoost"] / .f1["XGBoost"]))}\% for XGBoost, with the remaining distance to the rolling Stage~3
results (\Sexpr{tr_lag("LightGBM")} and \Sexpr{tr_lag("XGBoost")})
attributable to daily updating. The two components
therefore matter very differently across the pair: for LightGBM the
origin-available lag information and the daily updating contribute about
equally, whereas XGBoost gains almost everything from updating and little from
the lags available at the origin. Either way,
the Stage~3 gains are not an artefact of the rolling scheme. A substantial part
of them survives at a fixed origin.

\paragraph{Statistical Significance of the Main Comparisons.}

Every RMSE comparison so far compares two point estimates. This paragraph adds
a formal test for the two comparisons the results rely on most: the boosted
trees against Store-DOW, and XGBoost against LightGBM. Both use the same
Diebold--Mariano procedure, so the two hypotheses are tested in the same way.
The tests run on the daily mean squared errors of the primary holdout, 42
daily values per model. For two forecasts with squared-error loss series
$e^{(1)}_t$ and $e^{(2)}_t$, the daily loss differential is
$d_t = e^{(1)}_t - e^{(2)}_t$. The hypotheses have the form already stated in
Equation~\eqref{eq:dm_treevsstat}, $H_0: \mathbb{E}[d_t] = 0$ against
$H_1: \mathbb{E}[d_t] \neq 0$, and are tested with
\begin{equation}
    \mathrm{DM} = \frac{\bar{d}}{\sqrt{\widehat{\mathrm{Var}}(\bar{d})}},
    \qquad \bar{d} = \frac{1}{T}\sum_{t=1}^{T} d_t,
    \label{eq:dm}
\end{equation}
where $T=42$ is the number of daily losses. The archived scripts call
\texttt{forecast::dm.test} with \texttt{h=1}. With this setting the function
uses a lag-zero variance estimate, a small-sample correction and a $t_{41}$
reference distribution. It does not correct for serial correlation the way a
HAC estimator would. Daily losses from the same fixed origin can be
correlated, so the nominal significance levels should be read with care.

The archived statistics are \Sexpr{.e2 <- tr_read("stage6_e2.csv"); .dm <- setNames(.e2$DM, .e2$Comparison); tt_signed(.dm["LightGBM vs Store-DOW"], 2)} and \Sexpr{tt_signed(.dm["XGBoost vs Store-DOW"], 2)} for LightGBM and XGBoost
against Store-DOW, and \Sexpr{tt_num(.dm["LightGBM vs XGBoost"], 2)} for LightGBM against XGBoost. They come from this
original test setup and do not show that the result is robust to dependence
over time. The last comparison has nominal $p=\Sexpr{tt_num(.e2$p[.e2$Comparison == "LightGBM vs XGBoost"], 2)}$ but only a small RMSE
difference. The ARIMAX experiment instead uses paired seven-day
block-bootstrap intervals, and its short 42-day span is stated openly.

\paragraph{A test for the research question itself.} The six predictions in
Section~\ref{subsec:predictions} turn the research question into testable
statements, but the question can also be put to one direct test. Across
different stores, rather than across experimental conditions built
from the same pooled sample, does the boosted-tree advantage over Store-DOW
move with the predictable within-store variation $\mathrm{Var}(\delta_{s,t})$,
as the framework says it should? Section~\ref{sec:properties} answers this
with a per-store regression of each store's RMSE gain on measurable data
properties, among them the weekly-predictable share of variance and the
broader predictable share. Each coefficient is tested against
$H_0: \beta = 0$, meaning the property is unrelated to the gain, with
$H_1: \beta \neq 0$. The joint hypothesis $H_0: \beta_1=\dots=\beta_8=0$ is
tested with the omnibus $F$-test reported there. Table~\ref{tab:props_reg}
gives the coefficients and $t$-statistics; $|t|>1.96$ rejects the
per-coefficient $H_0$ at the 5\% level, and $R^2 = 0.26$ is the share of the
cross-store variation in gain that the regression explains. This is the test
that targets the research question most directly, because it runs over 1115
individual stores instead of the handful of aggregate conditions used
elsewhere in Section~\ref{sec:application}.

\paragraph{Replication uncertainty in the number-of-stores experiment.}
The values in parentheses in Table~\ref{tab:stage2_n} are standard errors of
the mean over the 20 draws, not standard deviations. For LightGBM they are
\Sexpr{.n <- tr_read("stage2_nstores.csv"); tt_num(.n$LGBM_delta_se[.n$N == 50], 2)},
\Sexpr{tt_num(.n$LGBM_delta_se[.n$N == 200], 2)} and
\Sexpr{tt_num(.n$LGBM_delta_se[.n$N == 500], 2)} percentage points at 50, 200
and 500 stores; multiplying by $\sqrt{20}$ gives the spread across draws. The
full panel is fitted once.

\paragraph{Hyperparameter Sensitivity of the LightGBM--XGBoost Ranking.}
\label{sec:grid}

The Stage~1 configurations differ in capacity, with 63 leaf-wise leaves
against depth-6 trees. A grid over XGBoost depth $\{6, 8, 10\}$ and LightGBM
leaves $\{31, 63, 127\}$, with the learning rate and early-stopping procedure
held fixed, tests whether the ranking between the two models survives other
capacity choices. Table~\ref{tab:grid} reports the no-lag results on the
primary holdout.

\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\caption{Hyperparameter grid on the primary holdout, no lag features,
ordered by RMSE. These are the archived Stage~6 results, a separate run from
Stage~1. Differences between repeated fits are not estimates of run-to-run
variance, because no controlled replication was run.}
\label{tab:grid}
\begin{tabular}{llr}
\toprule
Model & Capacity setting & RMSE \\
\midrule
<<tab-grid, results='asis', cache=FALSE>>=
d <- tr_read("hyperparameter_grid.csv")   # rossmann_stage6.Rmd, chunk e5
if (is.null(d)) {
  tt_missing(3, "hyperparameter_grid.csv", "rossmann_stage6.Rmd")
} else {
  for (i in seq_len(nrow(d))) {
    x <- d[i, ]
    # "max_depth = 8" -> "max depth $=$ 8"; "num_leaves = 63" -> "63 leaves".
    s <- x$Setting
    if (grepl("num_leaves", s)) {
      s <- paste(sub("^.*=\\s*", "", s), "leaves")
    } else {
      s <- gsub("_", " ", s); s <- sub("=", "$=$", s, fixed = TRUE)
    }
    cat(sprintf("%s & %s & %.0f \\\\\n", x$Model, s, x$RMSE))
  }
}
@
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The grid favours XGBoost across its three tested settings. The best result is an XGBoost
model at a \Sexpr{tr_grid_best("Setting")} (\Sexpr{tr_grid_best()}), and
\Sexpr{tr_grid_n_beat()} of the XGBoost settings overtake the widest LightGBM
configuration, although capacity helps only up to a point, since depth-8 edges
out depth-10. Two conclusions follow. First, the XGBoost--LightGBM ordering
reported in Stage~1 should be read as a comparison of common configurations,
not of algorithms. Capacity changes the margin, but this grid does not establish a reversal of
the Stage~1 XGBoost--LightGBM ordering. Second, the joint
advantage of the boosted ensembles over the baselines is robust across every
cell of the grid, since even the weakest configuration
(\Sexpr{tr_grid_worst()}) beats Store-DOW.

\paragraph{A Direct Test of the Framework.}
\label{sec:framework_test}

Equation~\eqref{eq:gap} predicts that the ensemble gain should increase with
the amount of predictable within-store variation, and this is testable directly
at the store level. For each store, the predictable-variation proxy is the variance of store-specific training
day-of-week and promotion cell means (in squared sales units), and the realised gain is the store's reduction in test MSE from
Store-DOW to LightGBM.

The cross-store correlation between the proxy and the realised MSE reduction
is $\Sexpr{.e4 <- read.csv(file.path(TT_DIR, "tab_e4_store_level_framework.csv")); .ct <- cor.test(.e4$var_explained, .e4$rmse_dow^2 - .e4$rmse_lgbm^2); tt_num(.ct$estimate, 2)}$
($p \Sexpr{if (.ct$p.value < 0.001) "< 0.001" else paste("=", tt_num(.ct$p.value, 3))}$, $n = \Sexpr{nrow(.e4)}$), so stores with more predictable variation
do gain more from the ensemble, as the framework predicts. The relationship is
still far from deterministic, since the proxy is coarse and explains only
about \Sexpr{sprintf("%.0f", 100 * .ct$estimate^2)}\% of the cross-store variance in gains. Its sign and size fit
the proposed mechanism, but they do not prove it causally, and store size
could still be partly behind it.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.68\textwidth]{fig_framework_test.pdf}
\caption{Store-level test of the framework: predictable within-store training
variance against the realised MSE reduction of LightGBM relative to
Store-DOW on the holdout.}
\label{fig:framework_test}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Scale with Fixed Composition and a Global--Local Comparison.}
\label{sec:global_local}

Two final checks return to the rejected scale prediction. First, the
number-of-stores experiment is repeated with samples drawn only from Type~A
stores, so that composition is held fixed while scale changes, extended up to the
full set of 602 Type~A stores. Under this design LightGBM's gain over Store-mean
rises overall and then approximately flattens, at \Sexpr{.e6 <- tr_read("stage6_e6.csv"); tt_num(.e6$gain_lgbm_mean[1])}\%, \Sexpr{tt_num(.e6$gain_lgbm_mean[2])}\%, \Sexpr{tt_num(.e6$gain_lgbm_mean[3])}\%, and
\Sexpr{tt_num(.e6$gain_lgbm_mean[4])}\% for \Sexpr{.e6$N[1]}, \Sexpr{.e6$N[2]}, \Sexpr{.e6$N[3]}, and the full \Sexpr{.e6$N[4]}-store set (standard errors \Sexpr{tt_num(.e6$gain_lgbm_se[1])}, \Sexpr{tt_num(.e6$gain_lgbm_se[2])},
and \Sexpr{tt_num(.e6$gain_lgbm_se[3])} over five draws for the sampled conditions). XGBoost moves the same
way, from \Sexpr{tt_num(.e6$gain_xgb_mean[1])}\% at \Sexpr{.e6$N[1]} stores to between \Sexpr{tt_num(min(.e6$gain_xgb_mean[-1]))}\% and \Sexpr{tt_num(max(.e6$gain_xgb_mean[-1]))}\% at the larger sizes, rather than the decline seen in the mixed samples. Much
of the downward pattern in Table~\ref{tab:stage2_n} therefore reflects changing
store composition rather than the number of series itself.

Second, a global LightGBM model is compared with separate per-store LightGBM
models on a common sample of 200 stores, using the no-lag features in both
cases. The local models reach an RMSE of \Sexpr{.e7 <- tr_read("stage6_e7.csv"); sprintf("%.0f", .e7$RMSE[grepl("^Local", .e7$Model)])} against \Sexpr{sprintf("%.0f", .e7$RMSE[grepl("^Global", .e7$Model)])} for the global
model restricted to the same stores. On this sample, fitting each store
separately is clearly better than pooling. This is consistent with the scale
results and suggests that learning across stores is not what drives the
boosted models' advantage in Rossmann, where each store already has around 760
training observations of its own. The comparison uses one fairly conservative
local setup and one global setup, so it is indicative, not complete,
but its direction matches the conclusion of \citet{monteromanso2021principles}
that the value of pooling depends on the data environment.

\subsubsection{Data Properties and Generalisability}
\label{sec:properties}

The experiments above vary operational settings: the number of stores, the
training length, the store-type composition, and the feature set, but any such
setting is by definition specific to Rossmann. To state a result that could
carry to another retail dataset, the advantage is therefore re-expressed in terms of \emph{measurable data
properties} of each store's series, the quantities that
Equation~\eqref{eq:gap} says should matter.

A finding such as ``more stores did not help'' is not meant to
transfer mechanically to another retailer. What carries over to other data is the
diagnosis: whether additional stores increase shared learnable structure,
whether the dominant variation is already captured by a seasonal baseline, and
whether the feature set makes the remaining predictable movements observable.

For every store, a small set of series-level properties is computed on the
training data: the coefficient of variation \texttt{cv}, the share of
within-store variance explained by store-specific day-of-week means
(\texttt{dow\_strength}), the share explained by day-of-week and promotion
jointly (\texttt{pred\_share}, a proxy for
$\mathrm{Var}(\delta)/\mathrm{Var}(y)$), a linear trend strength, the promotion
frequency, the closed-day fraction, the mean sales level, and the series length.
The realised gain of one global LightGBM over the Store-DOW baseline on the
42-day holdout is then regressed on these standardised properties. This is
implemented in \texttt{rossmann\_data\_properties.Rmd}. Across the 1115 stores
the gain over Store-DOW has a median of 28.8\% and a mean of 16.9\% (the mean is
pulled down by a thin tail of stores the global model fits poorly). The
regression explains about a quarter of the cross-store spread in this gain
($R^2 = 0.26$, $F_{8,1106} = 48.9$). Table~\ref{tab:props_reg} reports the
standardised coefficients. They are clearly signed and jointly significant, but
the modest $R^2$ matters for interpretation, because the remaining
three-quarters of the cross-store variation reflects estimation noise from the
single 42-day window, store-specific demand structure that the coarse property
set does not capture, and non-linear interactions a linear regression omits.
The property mapping should therefore be read as a tendency that orders stores
on average, not as a precise per-store predictor of the gain.

\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\caption{Per-store gain over Store-DOW (\%) regressed on standardised
series-level properties (1115 stores). Coefficients are comparable in size
because every predictor is standardised.}
\label{tab:props_reg}
\begin{tabular}{lrr}
\toprule
Property & Coefficient & $t$ \\
\midrule
<<tab-props, results='asis', cache=FALSE>>=
d <- tr_read("gain_vs_properties.csv")    # rossmann_data_properties.Rmd
if (is.null(d)) {
  tt_missing(3, "gain_vs_properties.csv", "rossmann_data_properties.Rmd")
} else {
  # Short glosses for the two properties whose names are not self-explanatory.
  gloss <- c(pred_share = " (Var$(\\delta)$ proxy)",
             cv = " (variability)",
             dow_strength = " (weekly share)")
  for (i in seq_len(nrow(d))) {
    x <- d[i, ]
    g <- if (x$Property %in% names(gloss)) gloss[[x$Property]] else ""
    cat(sprintf("\\texttt{%s}%s & $%s$ & $%s$ \\\\\n",
                gsub("_", "\\\\_", x$Property), g,
                sprintf("%+.2f", x$Coefficient), tt_num(x$t)))
  }
}
@
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The pattern refines the framework. The
predictable share \texttt{pred\_share}, the proxy for $\mathrm{Var}(\delta)$,
carries a large positive coefficient, so stores with more predictable
within-series variation gain more from the tree, consistent with
Equation~\eqref{eq:gap}. The coefficient on \texttt{dow\_strength} is large and
\emph{negative}. This is expected: the benchmark here is Store-DOW, which already captures pure
day-of-week structure, so a store whose predictability is mostly weekly leaves
little for the tree to add. The gain
therefore comes from predictable variation \emph{beyond} the weekly cycle, the
promotion and calendar interactions that \texttt{pred\_share} picks up but a
day-of-week mean cannot. Variability (\texttt{cv}) and a faint trend also widen
the gap, while the promotion frequency, closed-day fraction, and series length
are insignificant once these are controlled for. The one term not in
Equation~\eqref{eq:gap} is the sales level (\texttt{mean\_sales}), which enters
positively. Larger stores realise larger percentage gains even after the
coefficient of variation is held fixed, so the effect is more than high-volume
stores being less noisy in relative terms. A plausible channel is the global
objective itself: the pooled model minimises squared error in euros, so
high-volume stores carry more weight in the loss and the model fits their
recoverable component more precisely. That raises their realised $R_{\rm rec}$ and so
their gain, which means the level acts through the same recovery term already in
the framework rather than against it. It remains the one result the decomposition
does not predict directly, and isolating it on a dataset with a different sales
scale would be a useful check.

Because these properties can be measured on any retail series, the mapping from
properties to expected gain, rather than the Rossmann-specific RMSE numbers, is
the part of the finding that would transfer to a different dataset. A series with
high predictable share beyond its weekly pattern and high variability is where a
tree-based model is expected to pay off. One whose structure is almost entirely
weekly, and so already captured by a seasonal baseline, is not.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\IfFileExists{eda_outputs/fig_gain_vs_properties.pdf}{%
  \includegraphics[width=0.66\textwidth]{fig_gain_vs_properties.pdf}}{%
  \fbox{\parbox{0.8\textwidth}{\centering\vspace{2em}[Figure placeholder:
  standardised coefficients of the per-store gain regression. Export from
  \texttt{rossmann\_data\_properties.Rmd}.]\vspace{2em}}}}
\caption{Which data properties predict the tree advantage. Standardised
coefficients from a regression of the per-store percentage gain over Store-DOW
on the series-level properties.}
\label{fig:gain_props}
\end{figure}

\subsubsection{Direct Answer to the Research Question}

Table~\ref{tab:condition_summary} brings the separate experiments together.
The reported values measure the reduction in RMSE relative to Store-DOW, the
stronger of the two simple store-level baselines. Positive values therefore
mean that the tree-based ensemble outperforms a forecast that already accounts
for store identity and weekly seasonality. The table also includes the
conditions where the boosted models lose, the history-only feature set and,
for XGBoost, the six-month window, so the advantage is shown where it reverses
as well as where it holds. With only a store's own sales
history the boosted models trail Store-DOW by about 12\%, and the advantage
appears only once calendar, store, and promotion information is added, growing
further with sales lags. This fits the story of the M competitions, but it is
an illustration, not a proof of why the competitions differed.

\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\caption{Percentage reduction in RMSE relative to Store-DOW across the
experimental conditions. Positive values mean the boosted trees beat the
baseline. Negative values mark the settings where they fall behind it. The
history-only and calendar, store and promotion rows both come from Stage~5
(Table~\ref{tab:stage5}), so they use the same rows and the same origin-safe
history features. The lag-enriched row comes from Stage~3.}
\label{tab:condition_summary}
\small
\begin{tabular}{llrr}
\toprule
Dimension & Condition & XGBoost & LightGBM \\
\midrule
<<tab-conditions, results='asis', cache=FALSE>>=
# This table carries no new estimation. It restates the experiments above on a
# single scale: percentage reduction in RMSE relative to the Store-DOW
# baseline. Everything is recomputed from the same CSVs, so it cannot drift
# away from the tables it summarises.
d_n    <- tr_read("stage2_nstores.csv")
d_w    <- tr_read("stage2_window.csv")
d_t    <- tr_read("stage2_storetype.csv")
d_s5   <- tr_read("stage5_information_sets.csv")
d_lag  <- tr_read("stage3_lags.csv")
d_roll <- tr_read("stage4_rolling.csv")
d_dec  <- tr_read("rolling_december.csv")

row <- function(dim, cond, xg, lg)
  cat(sprintf("%s & %s & %s & %s \\\\\n", dim, cond, tt_signed(xg), tt_signed(lg)))
# Gain relative to Store-DOW, from two RMSEs.
gd <- function(dow, m) (dow - m) / dow * 100
# Gain relative to Store-DOW, from two gains that are both relative to the
# store-mean baseline.
gd_from_rel <- function(dow_rel, m_rel) (1 - (1 - m_rel / 100) / (1 - dow_rel / 100)) * 100

if (is.null(d_n)) {
  tt_missing(4, "stage2_nstores.csv", "rossmann_stages2_4.Rmd")
} else {
  for (i in seq_len(nrow(d_n))) {
    x <- d_n[i, ]
    row(if (i == 1) "Number of stores" else "",
        sprintf("%d stores", as.integer(x$N)),
        gd(x$StoreDOW_RMSE, x$XGB_RMSE), gd(x$StoreDOW_RMSE, x$LGBM_RMSE))
  }
  cat("\\addlinespace\n")
}
if (!is.null(d_w)) {
  for (i in seq_len(nrow(d_w))) {
    x <- d_w[i, ]
    lab <- if (identical(x$Window, "Full")) "Full history" else x$Window
    row(if (i == 1) "Training history" else "", lab,
        gd_from_rel(x$StoreDOW_delta, x$XGB_delta),
        gd_from_rel(x$StoreDOW_delta, x$LGBM_delta))
  }
  cat("\\addlinespace\n")
}
if (!is.null(d_t)) {
  nm <- c("A only" = "Type A", "B only" = "Type B",
          "C+D" = "C$+$D mix", "Full" = "Full sample")
  for (i in seq_len(nrow(d_t))) {
    x <- d_t[i, ]
    lab <- if (x$Condition %in% names(nm)) nm[[x$Condition]] else x$Condition
    row(if (i == 1) "Store composition" else "", lab,
        gd(x$StoreDOW_RMSE, x$XGB_RMSE), gd(x$StoreDOW_RMSE, x$LGBM_RMSE))
  }
  cat("\\addlinespace\n")
}
if (!is.null(d_s5) && !is.null(d_n) && !is.null(d_lag)) {
  h  <- d_s5[grepl("^1", d_s5[[1]]), ]                      # history only
  fl <- d_n[nrow(d_n), ]                                    # full feature set
  row("Feature information", "History only",
      h$Delta_DOW[h$Model == "XGBoost"], h$Delta_DOW[h$Model == "LightGBM"])
  f4 <- d_s5[grepl("^4", d_s5[[1]]), ]                     # all four Stage 5 sets
  row("", "$+$ calendar, store, promotion",
      f4$Delta_DOW[f4$Model == "XGBoost"], f4$Delta_DOW[f4$Model == "LightGBM"])
  row("", "$+$ lags and rolling means",
      d_lag$Delta_DOW[d_lag$Model == "XGBoost"],
      d_lag$Delta_DOW[d_lag$Model == "LightGBM"])
  cat("\\addlinespace\n")
}
if (!is.null(d_roll)) {
  for (i in seq_len(nrow(d_roll))) {
    x <- d_roll[i, ]
    row(if (i == 1) "Test period" else "", x$Window,
        gd(x$Store_DOW, x$XGBoost), gd(x$Store_DOW, x$LightGBM))
  }
  if (!is.null(d_dec)) {
    w <- d_dec[nrow(d_dec), ]
    row("", "Window 4 (Dec.)",
        gd(w$Store_DOW, w$XGBoost), gd(w$Store_DOW, w$LightGBM))
  }
}
@
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Both XGBoost and LightGBM outperform Store-DOW in every reported condition
except two: the history-only feature set, where both trail it by about 12\%,
and the six-month training window for XGBoost, the case explained in
Section~\ref{sec:subsets_results}. With six months LightGBM still beats
Store-DOW, and both models become clearly stronger once at least 12 months of
history are available. The largest full-sample gains come
after recent sales lags and rolling means are added, lifting XGBoost to \Sexpr{tr_lagd("XGBoost")}\%
over Store-DOW and LightGBM to \Sexpr{tr_lagd("LightGBM")}\%, while the number-of-stores experiment
moves in the opposite direction to the original scale prediction, so raw
cross-sectional size is not the condition that explains the advantage.

So the answer depends on data properties, not on the experimental levers: what decides the
gain is whether the series hold predictable variation the baseline leaves
unexplained and whether the feature set makes it observable. Section~\ref{sec:conclusion}
develops that reading against the six predictions.


\subsubsection{What the Experiments Establish}\label{sec:reach_evidence}
Table~\ref{tab:reach} separates what the experiments show about information
access from what they cannot show about hidden demand components. A lower loss
shows that a model used more information well; it does not identify which
unobserved part of demand it recovered.
\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Information access and interpretation limits.}\label{tab:reach}
\small
\begin{tabular}{p{2.7cm}p{4.9cm}p{4.9cm}}
\toprule
Comparison & Evidence & Interpretation limit \\
\midrule
Store-mean vs Store-DOW & Weekly conditioning lowers error. & Not a measure of all predictable demand. \\
History-only trees vs ARIMA & ARIMA wins in archived results. & History representation and recency differ. \\
Nested tree features & Calendar and commercial features improve fitted models. & No causal effect or universal family ranking. \\
Updated lag features & Recent observations improve one-step forecasts. & Different information from fixed-origin forecasting. \\
Matched covariates & Section~\ref{sec:arimax_check} compares ARIMAX and trees. & Two origins and 50 eligible stores limit generalisation. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}


