% ============================================================
\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}
% ============================================================

Table~\ref{tab:predictions_summary} summarises the outcome of the six
predictions before the detailed discussion.

\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\caption{Summary of the framework's predictions and the empirical outcomes.}
\label{tab:predictions_summary}
\small
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{4pt}
\renewcommand{\arraystretch}{0.92}
\begin{tabular}{l >{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{4.3cm} l >{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{4.3cm}}
\toprule
\# & Prediction & Outcome & Key evidence \\
\midrule
1 & Gain increases with the number of stores & Rejected &
Relative gain falls significantly as stores are added
(Table~\ref{tab:stage2_n}) \\
2 & Gain increases with training length & Partially supported &
Large gain from 6 to 12 months; flat thereafter
(Table~\ref{tab:stage2_window}) \\
3 & Homogeneous store groups show smaller gains & Not supported &
Type~B has the largest gains and the largest average weekly share; the latter
does not by itself explain the incremental gain
(Tables~\ref{tab:stage2_type}, \ref{tab:storetypes}) \\
4 & Lag features improve the boosted models & Supported &
RMSE falls \Sexpr{tr_rmse("XGBoost")}$\to$\Sexpr{tr_lag("XGBoost")} (XGBoost)
and \Sexpr{tr_rmse("LightGBM")}$\to$\Sexpr{tr_lag("LightGBM")} (LightGBM)
(Table~\ref{tab:stage3}) \\
5 & Regularised ensembles outperform simpler trees & Partially supported &
Boosted trees take the top two, but un-regularised bagging is third and beats the
random forest (Table~\ref{tab:stage1}) \\
6 & Advantage grows as the information environment is enriched & Supported &
History only trails Store-DOW and Auto-ARIMA; full features overtake both
(Table~\ref{tab:stage5}) \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

This thesis asked which measurable properties of retail demand data explain
when tree-based ensembles beat simple baselines. Rossmann gives a clear
answer: the advantage comes from predictable variation that the baseline
leaves unexplained and that the feature set makes visible. The baseline
comparison is the first part of that answer. Store-DOW cuts RMSE by 13.9\%
against Store-mean, so a large part of Rossmann demand can already be
forecast from store identity and the weekly pattern. Even against this harder
benchmark, XGBoost and LightGBM cut RMSE by about 39\% without lag features on
the primary Stage~1 holdout; the exact margin differs across the other windows
and separately executed runs. Their success is therefore not an artefact of a
weak store-mean benchmark. It comes from predictable structure beyond the
weekly pattern. When the models are given only a store's own sales history,
they fall behind both Store-DOW and Auto-ARIMA, which shows that a flexible
algorithm alone is not enough.

The ARIMAX check puts a limit on this conclusion. With the same covariates,
ARIMAX keeps up with LightGBM at one origin and loses at the other, and
shorter training histories do not change this: it is not significantly better
than LightGBM in any combination of origin and window. The conclusion is
therefore conditional. Trees can use structure beyond a seasonal baseline, but
whether they beat statistical forecasting can only be judged when both get the
same covariates and the same forecast origins. The univariate comparison on
its own cannot show that.

\subsection{Reconciling the Rejected and Unsupported Predictions}
\label{sec:reconcile}

Two of the six predictions fail: Prediction~1 (scale) is rejected, and
Prediction~3 (heterogeneity) is not supported. Both were stated in terms of operational levers,
the number of stores and an administrative store-type label, and in both cases
the lever turns out to be a poor proxy for the quantity in
Equation~\eqref{eq:gap} it was meant to track. The decomposition itself
remains an organising identity, not a causal mechanism that has been
independently confirmed. These two results are a real challenge to the
initial hypotheses. Store composition is a plausible explanation for them, not
proof that the mechanism survives every test, and the property regressions are
an exploratory refinement that still needs independent validation.

The experiments on scale, training length, and store type clarify what the
relevant data properties are not. More cross-sectional size does not
automatically lift a global tree model's relative performance, since both
boosted models gain more in the smaller store subsets than in the full mixed
sample (Table~\ref{tab:stage2_n}). The fixed-composition and global--local checks in
Section~\ref{sec:global_local} sharpen this point, since LightGBM's gain stops
falling when composition is held fixed and per-store models beat the pooled
model on a common sample. Training length also matters only up to a point:
results are weakest at six months, jump at twelve, and then flatten
(Table~\ref{tab:stage2_window}), suggesting that Rossmann needs one full annual
cycle but gains little from older observations beyond that. The store-type
experiment points in the same direction, since the supposedly homogeneous
Type~B subset yields the largest gains rather than the smallest, despite its
strong weekly baseline. It also has the largest average weekly share in
Table~\ref{tab:storetypes}, but that alone does not explain why the trees gain
so much there. So neither the number of series, the length of the history nor
an administrative label is the explanation that carries over. Each matters
only through the predictable structure and baseline strength it brings with
it.

Feature information is the strongest and most stable driver. Adding lags and
rolling means drops both boosted models far below the baselines
(Table~\ref{tab:stage3}), and these variables account for most fitted-model
importance once they are available. The incremental information experiment ties
this result back to the literature review: with only a few old summaries of
their own history, XGBoost and LightGBM lose clearly to per-store Auto-ARIMA.
Once calendar variables are added they beat the statistical benchmarks
clearly, and the calendar indicators give their largest single gain. The path
of the M competitions, where statistical methods held their ground until tasks
with rich, aligned covariates appeared, therefore repeats inside one retail
dataset when the algorithm and sample are fixed and only the information set
changes. This is the central empirical thread of the thesis: tree ensembles
win when the feature set exposes recent and recurring demand movements, not
whenever the dataset is larger or the algorithm more flexible.

Between the two boosted implementations there is no stable winner: their RMSE
difference changes across separately executed fits and test windows. The grid
cannot pick a best model either, because every cell is scored on the same
holdout, so choosing the winner there would build in selection bias. Rossmann
thus reproduces the broad M5 finding that boosted trees work very well for
tabular retail forecasting, while showing that the gap between the two
implementations depends on the chosen configurations rather than on the
algorithms.

The evidence, taken together, gives no support to a tidy rule that tree-based
methods win because a dataset is larger, longer, or more heterogeneous. In
Rossmann the deciding condition is whether the model sees variables that capture
recent and recurring demand patterns. A long enough history and a meaningful
cross-section are needed for estimation, but past those minimums, feature
relevance counts for more than raw dataset size. The Stage~1 comparison
reaches the same verdict against dedicated statistical models on the primary
holdout, where LightGBM and XGBoost beat Auto-ARIMA, Theta, and ETS and Random
Forest finishes clearly ahead of Auto-ARIMA. The broader result is strongest for the two
boosted ensembles, not for tree-based methods as an undifferentiated
group.

\subsection{Practical Implications}

For a practitioner with a few hundred to a few thousand store-level series and
standard calendar and promotion information, several things follow. Compute a
store and day-of-week average before crediting any complex model, since it
removes about 14\% of the store-mean error at almost no cost and makes weak
models easy to spot. Feature work then pays off before model work, since sales lags and rolling
means produced larger gains than any change in dataset size and about three quarters of
the fitted models' importance lands on them. Roughly one full year of history is
enough in this setting, and gathering more stores or longer histories will not
raise relative performance by itself. Finally, check model advantages over
several forecast origins, because their size, but not their direction, drifts
across test windows.

\subsection{Limitations and Future Work}

The conclusions hold for the designs that were fitted. The ARIMAX comparisons
deal with one important information confound, but they cover only 50 eligible
stores and two origins. The full-panel tables are read from the executed
reports of the individual analysis scripts, which were run separately rather
than together in one computing environment. Integer coding of categories,
imputation before the split and the different ways the history is represented
remain limits of those archived comparisons.

There are further limitations. The evidence comes from one dataset of
aggregate store sales, so the same framework should be run on product-level
data such as a subset of M5, where demand is more intermittent. Stage~3 uses
rolling one-day-ahead updates, so its lag gains are not fixed-origin 42-day
results; the recursive analysis in Section~\ref{sec:additional} addresses
this. The LightGBM--XGBoost comparison sets two fitted specifications against
each other under one tuning budget, and the grid in Section~\ref{sec:grid}
shows that their order depends on capacity, even though their joint advantage
over the baselines does not. The Type~B result rests on only 17 stores. Every
forecast here is a point forecast, so the probabilistic evaluation that was
central to the M5 Uncertainty track is left out. Finally, most comparisons
rest on a single 42-day holdout, so the headline RMSE rankings are point
estimates without formal standard errors. The rolling windows of Stage~4 and
the cross-store regression in Section~\ref{sec:properties} protect the main
claims against a single-window artefact, but a fuller treatment would use many
more rolling origins and attach uncertainty to every reported number.
Estimating the store-level link between predictable variation and realised
gain (Section~\ref{sec:framework_test}) on several datasets would test whether
recent demand features stay central in more intermittent and more detailed
retail settings.

